\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Rises As Shorts Covered Pre-Election  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2004\par 06:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar rose against the yen and the euro in Asia Monday as speculators unwound short positions built up last week to take quick profits ahead of Tuesday's U.S. presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 A videotape aired Friday by Arabic television channel Al-Jazeera showing al-Qaida leader Osama bin Laden threatening the United States initially dampened sentiment for the dollar, putting it under selling pressure in early trading, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 But few were willing to push the U.S. unit down aggressively ahead of the election. Instead, they bought it back on worries of getting caught off guard by any brief dollar rally if incumbent George W. Bush wins, an outcome widely viewed as more favorable for the dollar than a win by Democratic Senator John Kerry, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market has already factored in weak-dollar scenarios like a victory by Kerry or a dead-even race in which the final outcome won't come out quickly," said Akifumi Uchida, senior manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade at Sumitomo Trust & Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "But there's a chance for Bush to stage a clear victory, and traders mindful of this were covering (dollar) shorts," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts, however, believe any dollar rally will be short-lived given a host of dollar-bearish factors, such as signs of a slowdown in the U.S. economy and renewed market attention to the nation's massive trade and fiscal deficits.  \par \par \uc2 "My main scenario is that the dollar will stage a full-fledged decline after bouncing back modestly after the presidential election," Uchida said, noting there's "plenty of chance" for the greenback to test the March 31 low of Y103.40 shortly after the election.  \par \par \uc2 Uchida also thinks it's "only a matter of time" before the euro breaks above $1.30. "If the current weak-dollar momentum continues, the euro may test $1.30 this week," but if speculators take a breather and decide to buy back dollars for profit-taking, a break above this level may be delayed possibly until the end of this year, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High \par    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              106.23-29  105.77-83  +0.43  106.31  105.78   -1.03 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2775-81  1.2802-08  -0.21  1.2830  1.2776   +1.42 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.8343-53  1.8387-91  -0.24  1.8413  1.8351   +2.69 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.1974-80  1.1945-51  +0.24  1.1980  1.1914   -3.40 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.2189-94  1.2174-77  +0.12  1.2194  1.2181   -6.19 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.7469-74  0.7484-89  -0.20  0.7499  0.7473   -0.84 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.6849-59  0.6848-58  +0.01  0.6877  0.6765   +4.56 \par USD/DEM Deutsche Mark    2.2150-75  2.2150-75   0.00  2.2157  2.2160   -0.11 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-11-04 0540GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data released Monday showed speculators' net yen-long positions soared to 27,636 contracts in the week to Oct. 26 from 8,565 contracts the week before. It was the largest volume of yen longs since the week to Feb. 17, so the unwinding of these positions may briefly send the dollar higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That has led J.P. Morgan Chase Bank of Tokyo to recommend unwinding dollar-short, yen-long positions for now and begin selling the U.S. unit again when it bounces back above Y107.00.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the medium- to long-term, our expectations for a weaker dollar haven't changed, but given the recent buildup of yen longs and the events calendar this week, we recommend buying back shorts," said Tohru Sasaki, the bank's chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar, however, likely won't find much support other than such position-adjustment, with little chance for Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to step into the market to stem the yen's rise at current levels, traders say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With an end to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's deflation in sight, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , if there is any, will come below Y105.00," said Daisuke Uno, market analyst at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking, noting that the chance of MOF action will increase only when the dollar falls below its March 31 low of Y103.40.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Uno expects the dollar to test its Feb. 11 low of Y105.16 this week and target Y100-Y105 by the end of this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Hugh Lawson \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-11-04 0625GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041101e0b100055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
