\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Option Views: Antsy USD/JPY Means Stark Gamma Choice  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2004\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Russell Floyd \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The options market is growing increasingly nervous as the dollar grinds lower against the yen, leaving traders with a stark choice on how they want to approach the market's growing risks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 If you think the Japanese authorities will again make good on their threats and manage to support the dollar, it makes sense to sell strikes and place triggers below the market. But for traders expecting dollar support to give way, simply owning dollar/yen volatility here is a good bet.  \par \par \uc2 Amid global dollar weakness on such concerns as high oil prices and the giant U.S. budget and trade deficits, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped last week below Y106 for the first time since April. The dollar bobbed back off its lows and around 0555 GMT Monday was at Y106.26, but USD/JPY risk reversals are their most dollar-bearish in seven months.  \par \par \uc2 The options market is also antsy ahead of Tuesday's U.S. presidential election and Friday's U.S. job data, on top of turbulence last week over China's interest rate hike.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar's best friend of the past couple of years, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is again hinting it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the strong yen from derailing a still-fragile economic recovery. "If we judge that moves are out of line (with fundamentals), we will take the necessary action," Finance Minster Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Betting with the MOF was often a smart move in the 15 months through March, when the ministry sold a record Y35 trillion, almost all of it to prop up the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 We discussed last week two strategies that profit from a cushion on the USD/JPY downside - a reverse knock-out USD put/JPY call with a trigger around 103.5, and a zero-premium ratio yen call spread that is net short-gamma below the market.  \par \par \uc2 But both these trades share a glaring weakness: vulnerability to surprise.  \par \par \uc2 For the reverse knock-out, if the trigger is hit, you lose a very in-the-money option. For the ratio yen call spread, a huge dislocation lower could be devastating; the risk is unlimited as you would have sold more out-of-the-money options than you bought at-the-money-forward options.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        Short MOF, Structure For USD/JPY Downside \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Generally, we prefer trades that are net long-volatility over those with a negative exposure to sudden moves. Some traders rake in decay day after day for months, only to have all the accumulated profit wiped out by one sudden move against them.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That situation could be shaping up again in USD/JPY, and we prefer the other side of this risk profile, aiming to structure trades that bleed slightly but that will capture the sudden, random and often dislocating moves that can develop in exchange rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Having been burned on downside-volatility plays before, traders might be tempted to lean toward selling this area of the distribution. But we would prefer to sit long-gamma from here, positioning in the three-month for a few weeks to see if something comes from the recent spot range break. Off a 9.60% volatility, a buyer of a three-month straddle needs to capture about Y4 in movement via delta hedging to break even. The dollar fell Y4.25 last month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Current market pricing indicates tension in the pair is building.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The volatility implied by one-week at-the-money-forward USD/JPY options traded 12.25% Friday, a four-month high and among the richest implied volatilities among the major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week at 1.20 standard deviations above its one-year mean.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The one-month implied, up another 0.95 percentage point last week to 9.95%, is at its highest since Aug. 2 and is above its one-year moving average (now 9.66%) for the first time since July. The three-month was up 0.75 vol to 9.60%, and the one-year picked up 0.48 vol to 9.38%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And yet, actual volatility languishes. The three-month historical volatility fell 0.38 vol last week to 6.04%. That leaves it a full 3.56 vols below the three-month implied - the most expensive risk premium among the major pairs last week with a Z-score of 1.95.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In another sign of increasing tension, the one-month 25-delta risk reversal favored USD puts/JPY calls over USD calls/JPY by 1.65% Friday, widening from 1.2% for yen calls a week earlier. It's now reflecting the strongest bias for yen calls since early April, when the dollar was hovering under Y106 after testing briefly below Y104. As long as pressure on USD/JPY stays, the risk reversal should still favor yen calls.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                   Long USD/CAD Gamma \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders looking for outright gamma might also consider structuring in the U.S. dollar/Canadian dollar market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While USD/JPY shows potential, USD/CAD has already been providing real gamma profits, showing a five-day close-to-close volatility of 11.14% on Friday, while one-month actual volatility was up 1.49 vols on the week to 9.05%. With one-month implied volatility quoted 8.95% mid-market, there was a mere 0.1% risk premium Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The USD/CAD options market is also pricing a heightened perception of risk in the spot market. The one-week implied traded 10.85% Friday in New York, 2.10 standard deviations above its one-year mean of 9.0%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk reversals are biased in favor of CAD calls by 0.25% in the one-month to 0.30% in the one-year. It is a somewhat extreme bias in the one-month, where the last year has seen a greater preference for CAD calls only 12% of the time. Risk reversals for CAD calls are also cheap, with one-month to the one-year comprising four of the 10 cheapest of all pairs last week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CAD implieds also look cheap compared to other pairs.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To judge by actual volatility, USD/CAD is one of the least stable of the major pairs. One-month actual EUR/USD volatility was 7.95% on Friday, USD/CHF was 9.97% and AUD/USD was 8.04%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But looking at implied volatilities, it seems the market is not pricing USD/CAD accordingly. One-month implied EUR/USD traded 9.90%, USD/CHF was 10.75% and AUD/USD traded 11.43%. At 8.95%, the one-month USD/CAD implied seems like a real bargain.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Running long gamma in this pair could prove rewarding. Consider one- to three-month straddles struck at the current forward rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     -By Russell Floyd, russell.floyd@vollab.com \par  \par    (Russell Floyd is an independent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options strategist who may have positions in some of the products discussed above. Opinions expressed are those of the author, not of Dow Jones Newswires.) \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market Data provided by GFI Group, NY ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.gfigroup.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.gfigroup.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ) \par    Option Pricing by Fenics \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  provided by GFI Group, NY ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.gfi