\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar under pressure, falls to 6-month low vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2004\par 10:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 29 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a six-month low against the yen and drifted near recent eight-month lows versus the euro on Friday as investors considered the effect of China's interest rate hike on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts thought the rate hike was unlikely to affect global economic growth much. But they were divided over the move's implications for the timing of any future revaluation of the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 A revaluation is broadly expected to put downward pressure on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The main theme today is continuation of the recent trend, meaning dollar weakness," said Bilal Hafeez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The rate rise in China on the margin increases the risk of people revising downward their global growth forecasts, but we aren't revising ours. And it does not tell you anything about revaluation." Hafeez also said markets were now turning toward U.S. gross domestic product (GDP) data due at 1230 GMT for new clues about growth momentum.  \par \par \uc2 A weak figure could give the dollar another push lower towards this week's eight-month lows of $1.2841 per euro.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT the dollar stood slightly weaker on the day at $1.2761 per euro. It hit its lowest level in six months at 105.82 yen, falling about a third of a percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 WHAT KIND OF GROWTH?  \par \par \uc2 Markets were taken by surprise on Thursday when China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would raise rates, in an effort to cool its red-hot economy.  \par \par \uc2 The yen and commodity-exporting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar initially sank on worries that slower Chinese growth would hurt their economies, giving the dollar a lift.  \par \par \uc2 But with analysts divided over which economy, including the the United States, would be hurt the most from a possible slowdown in China, many traders said it was too early to draw conclusions.  \par \par \uc2 "The market doesn't know how this China issue will affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mitsuo Imaizumi, deputy general manager of the international bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  department at Daiwa Securities SMBC.  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan trades in a narrow band at around 8.28 to the dollar and is widely believed to be undervalued. Beijing has been under pressure from the United States and, increasingly, Europe, to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Japanese authorities appeared reluctant to step into the market despite the yen's decline, with many analysts arguing the coming U.S. election was keeping them at bay.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan governor Toshihiko Fukui said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were not a risk to the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "His comments suggest we are not seeing the line in the sand yet. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the U.S. election," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HBOS.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures for October at 1000 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. ELECTIONS LOOM  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts argue that it may not matter to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets which candidate wins the presidential elections in the U.S. next week.  \par \par \uc2 But they said the market was cautious about the possibility of a drawn-out process to establish the winner, similar to the five-week battle in 2000 which eventually awarded the White House to George W. Bush. "I think you've got to look at the possibility that like last time we won't get a quick result, and if the process becomes drawn out and confused, people will likely sell the dollar," said Toru Umemoto, research associate at the Global Security Research Centre at Keio University.  \par \par \uc2 "But whether Bush or Kerry wins, the outlook is for an economic slowdown and we can expect a weaker dollar," said Umemoto, who expects the dollar to challenge 100 yen and the euro to test $1.30 by year-end.  \par \par \uc2 The latest Reuters/Zogby poll showed Bush leading Democratic rival John Kerry by two points nationally.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041029e0at006nu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
