\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar softens in run-up to U.S. elections.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Plumberg  \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2004\par 19:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 29 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped on Friday as generally positive U.S. economic data failed to inspire much buying interest ahead of next week's U.S. presidential elections and October payrolls report.  \par \par \uc2 Deep-seated market concerns over the record U.S. current account deficit continue to arouse a sense of bearishness on the dollar, traders say.  \par \par \uc2 They are also apprehensive about the possibility that the election could end up in an electoral college tie or a protracted legal battle like 2000 in which case the winner may not be known for weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Americans go to the polls on Tuesday. The latest Reuters/Zogby opinion poll has U.S. President George W. Bush and challenger Sen. John Kerry in a dead heat.  \par \par \uc2 "That's just such a potent week or so that people are reluctant to turn around the trend of the past few months and buy dollars aggressively," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with IDEAglobal in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar initially strengthened after the Chicago Purchasing Management Index, a key gauge of factory activity in the central region of the United States, rose to a five-month high of 68.5 in October from 61.9 in September.  \par \par \uc2 Among the components of the index, the production index was the highest since August 1950 and the new orders reading was the highest in two decades.  \par \par \uc2 "These numbers are remarkably good and should be dollar supportive," said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 "But ahead of the election, it is unlikely that we will see the dollar get too strong," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had eased earlier on lower-than-forecast U.S. growth data. Real gross domestic product rose 3.7 percent in the July through September quarter, a faster pace than the 3.3 percent registered in the prior three-month period but well short of the 4.2 percent clip forecast by economists.  \par \par \uc2 In early afternoon trading in New York, the euro hit a session high of $1.2786, up around 0.3 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Japanese yen, the dollar fell around 0.3 percent to 105.93 yen. The unit was also down 0.4 percent to 1.1956 Swiss francs.  \par \par \uc2 VOTES, JOBS AND RATES  \par \par \uc2 In addition to Tuesday's U.S. election, traders are keeping a eye on the October employment report due at the end of the week.  \par \par \uc2 At this point, the market is expecting an increase of 150,000 new jobs this month. That number, which is largely acts as a proxy of the health of the U.S. economy, could give markets a better idea of whether the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates in December.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the dollar would need to see some good economic news and sustained upward pressure on interest rates for it to really gather much momentum," said Lisa Finstrom, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Citigroup Global Markets Inc. in New York.  \par \par \uc2 If the dollar continues to fall precipitously against yen and the euro next week, dealers will listen keenly for comments from European and Japanese policy makers.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, authorities appeared reluctant to step into the market despite the yen's decline, with many analysts arguing the coming U.S. election was keeping them at bay.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the four weeks ended Oct. 27 despite the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were not a risk to the economy. The dollar slipped to fresh six-month lows around 105.80 yen following Fukui's comments.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041029e0at00byn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
