\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dlr hits 6-mth low vs yen, eyes U.S. GDP, election.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2004\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 29 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a six-month low against the yen and drifted above recent eight-month lows versus the euro on Friday as investors awaited U.S. economic growth data and next week's presidential elections.  \par \par \uc2 The growth numbers, due at 1230 GMT, are expected to show the U.S. economy accelerating in the third quarter to an annualised pace of 4.2 percent from 3.3 percent in the previous quarter.  \par \par \uc2 This could be good news for the dollar, which has been under heavy pressure in recent weeks and fell to an eight month low of $1.2841 per euro on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 But investors are cautious ahead of next week's presidential polls, with many worried that the result of the closely-contested vote may not be immediately known.  \par \par \uc2 Markets are also still pondering the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact of China's unexpected interest rate hike, the first one in nine years.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is still generally in a negative dollar mood," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The Chinese news has been discounted and people are focusing on the GDP data and elections."  \par \par \uc2 At 1143 GMT the dollar was steady on the day against the euro at $1.2744, after hitting day's lows at $1.2773 earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The euro got a brief push higher after euro zone data showed inflation in the 12-nation region surged to 2.5 percent in October, further above the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal of below but close to 2.0 percent, due to soaring oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit its lowest level in six months at 105.82 yen, falling about a third of a percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 UNCERTAINTY IN ASIA  \par \par \uc2 Markets were taken by surprise on Thursday when China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would raise rates, in an effort to cool its red-hot economy.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts thought the rate hike was unlikely to affect global economic growth much.  \par \par \uc2 But they were divided over the move's implications for the timing of any future revaluation of the Chinese yuan, which could be seen largely as dollar-negative.  \par \par \uc2 "The main theme today is continuation of the recent trend, meaning dollar weakness," said Bilal Hafeez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The rate rise in China on the margin increases the risk of people revising downward their global growth forecasts, but we aren't revising ours. And it does not tell you anything about revaluation."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Japanese authorities appeared reluctant to step into the market despite the yen's decline, with many analysts arguing the coming U.S. election was keeping them at bay.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan governor Toshihiko Fukui said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were not a risk to the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "His comments suggest we are not seeing the line in the sand yet. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the U.S. election," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HBOS.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the four weeks ended Oct. 27 despite the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 AND IN THE U.S.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts argue that it may not matter to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets which candidate wins the presidential elections in the U.S. next week.  \par \par \uc2 But they said the market was cautious about the possibility of a drawn-out process to establish the winner, similar to the five-week battle in 2000 which eventually awarded the White House to George W. Bush.  \par \par \uc2 The latest Reuters/Zogby poll showed Bush in a dead heat with Democratic rival John Kerry.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041029e0at0089p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
