\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar eyes on US outlook, hits 6-mth low vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nima Elbagir  \par \uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2004\par 13:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 29 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a six-month low against the yen and drifted near recent eight-month lows versus a rising euro on Friday as investors considered the effect of China's interest rate hike on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also got a brief push higher after euro zone data showed inflation in the 12-nation region surged to 2.5 percent in October, further above the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal, due to soaring oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 And investors were gearing up for U.S. gross domestic product data due at 1230 GMT for hints about the pace of economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "The main theme today is continuation of the recent trend, meaning dollar weakness," said Bilal Hafeez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The rate rise in China on the margin increases the risk of people revising downward their global growth forecasts, but we aren't revising ours. And it does not tell you anything about revaluation."  \par \par \uc2 At 1025 GMT the dollar was steady on the day against the euro at $1.2744, after hitting day's lows at $1.2773 earlier.  \par \par \uc2 "This (inflation data) has for the first time in a long time raised the prospect of an ECB rate hike," said Chris Gothard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Brown Brothers Harriman in London.  \par \par \uc2 However, bond markets did not show a rise in rate hike expectations from the ECB.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also hit its lowest level in six months at 105.82 yen, falling about a third of a percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 LOOKING AT CHINA  \par \par \uc2 Markets were taken by surprise on Thursday when China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would raise rates, in an effort to cool its red-hot economy.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts thought the rate hike was unlikely to affect global economic growth much. But they were divided over the move's implications for the timing of any future revaluation of the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Japanese authorities appeared reluctant to step into the market despite the yen's decline, with many analysts arguing the coming U.S. election was keeping them at bay.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan governor Toshihiko Fukui said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were not a risk to the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "His comments suggest we are not seeing the line in the sand yet. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the U.S. election," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HBOS.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the four weeks ended Oct. 27 despite the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 AND THE U.S.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts argue that it may not matter to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets which candidate wins the presidential elections in the U.S. next week.  \par \par \uc2 But they said the market was cautious about the possibility of a drawn-out process to establish the winner, similar to the five-week battle in 2000 which eventually awarded the White House to George W. Bush.  \par \par \uc2 "I think you've got to look at the possibility that like last time we won't get a quick result, and if the process becomes drawn out and confused, people will likely sell the dollar," said Toru Umemoto, research associate at the Global Security Research Centre at Keio University.  \par \par \uc2 "But whether Bush or (Senator John) Kerry wins, the outlook is for an economic slowdown and we can expect a weaker dollar," said Umemoto, who expects the dollar to challenge 100 yen and the euro to test $1.30 by year-end.  \par \par \uc2 The latest Reuters/Zogby poll showed Bush leading Democratic rival John Kerry by two points nationally.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041029e0at007ql\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
