\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says no  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  Sept 29-Oct 27.  \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2004\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the four weeks ended Oct. 27, despite the yen's steep rise to a six-month high against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry, worried about the impact of a strong yen on Japan's export-led recovery, last stepped into the market to sell the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 16 when the yen jumped to around 108.60 to the dollar .  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities have stayed out of the market since then even as the dollar hit a four-year low of 103.40 yen at the end of March before rising to 115 in mid-May.  \par \par \uc2 At 1000 GMT on Friday, the dollar was trading near a fresh six-month low of 105.91 . The ministry statement covered the period from Sept. 29 to Oct. 27.  \par \par \uc2 Pressured by concerns about the U.S. economy and a yawning trade deficit, the dollar fell 4.6 percent between the Sept. 29 high of 111.40 to a then six-month low of 106.22 on Oct. 27.  \par \par \uc2 Alarmed by the yen's recent rise, Japanese authorities have again started to warn the market that action will be taken if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates get out of kilter with economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the Finance Ministry is likely to stay out of the market until the dollar dropped closer towards 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 JP Morgan said in a report on Friday it did not expect any Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the dollar fell to around 103 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities spent an annual record 20 trillion yen ($188.2 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in calendar 2003 and about 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004. ($1=106.24 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041029e0at007hj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
