\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Midday  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : China's Rate Rise Helps Dollar For Now -2-  \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2004\par 22:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 Oct 28, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The report from Beijing came soon after the dollar had tried to stage a recovery from a morning low on a large commercial order through a U.S. agent bank. Market sources noted that the order had initially looked liked Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and led to fears that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had come into the market to stop the dollar from falling under Y106.00. Earlier, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen as far as Y106.01, despite a sharp reversal in crude oil prices, which would normally be taken as positive for the U.S. economy. The Chinese news only helped to drive the price of crude even lower, with futures on the New York Mercantile Exchange being quoted at $51.57 a barrel just after midday. This was up from the low of $51.29 hit during Asia trading but still well below $52.36 at the close in New York Wednesday. The yen's early advance was also made against a background of mixed news. While Japanese stocks had firmed, pushing the Nikkei index up 161 points on the day, new economic data showed Japan's industrial output putting in a much worse performance than the market had expected. Instead of expanding 0.5% as forecast, production contracted 0.7% last month and made it even more likely that the Bank of Japan will move to stop the yen from rising too far. However, some experts feel that the bank is unlikely to enter the market until next Tuesday's election in the U.S., as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now could easily be construed as a political move. At 1230 GMT, the dollar is trading back up at Y106.82, well above Y106.55 late in New York Wednesday. The better trend spilled over into the euro, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losing some of its early strength and sinking back to $1.2666 from $1.2696. The dollar was also up slightly at CHF1.2086 from CHF1.2079. The pound was trading down at $1.8220 from $1.8278, its slide helped by news from the British Bankers' Association that mortgage approvals were down 30% in the year to September and a new survey from Nationwide showing that house prices were down 0.4% last month.  \par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041030e0as000gr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
