\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sags as market digests China move, ECB.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2004\par 20:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 28 (Reuters) - The dollar weakened broadly on Thursday, reversing a short-lived rally on news China had raised interest rates as the market decided Beijing's move did not radically change market fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Comments by an unnamed European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy source that no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the euro's strength will be forthcoming unless market moves become more "extreme" also lent support to the euro, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "If those comments are true, they are significant," said Paresh Upadhyaya, portfolio manager with Putnam Investments in Boston. "I do think (the euro's subsequent bounce) makes sense ... they (the ECB) may tolerate a move up to $1.30."  \par \par \uc2 The news came after the market had generally settled down in the wake of China's first rate hike in almost a decade.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had initially rallied as dealers thought the tightening would adversely affect Asian and commodity-exporting countries, and that it might be instead of a yuan revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar eased as most economists decided commodity exporters were unlikely to be devastated by declining demand and that the rate hike wouldn't alter Beijing's timetable - whenever that may be - to loosen its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "We continue to believe China will take its time," said John Rothfield, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in San Francisco.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market's initial response to China's announcement was to buy back dollars that had been sold short in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar quickly weakened as traders refocused on the factors that have been weighing on it recently, namely mixed U.S. economic data, low U.S. interest rates, a growing current account gap and uncertainty ahead of Tuesday's presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 By midafternoon in New York, the euro was up 0.2 percent at $1.2731. The dollar was down half a percent against the Swiss franc at 1.2001 francs.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of commodity-exporting countries recovered smartly after earlier getting pounded. The Australian dollar was up a touch at $0.7449 while the greenback was off around 0.25 percent against the Canadian dollar at C$1.2223.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was off at 106.16 yen.  \par \par \uc2 INTERPRETING CHINA  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the benchmark one-year yuan lending rate to 5.58 percent from 5.31 percent, and the rate on one-year deposits to 2.25 percent from 1.98 percent in an effort to cool its red-hot economy.  \par \par \uc2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York, said the rate hike "takes off the table" a near-term change in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg but helps set the stage for a revaluation in future.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a limit to how high they can raise rates without negatively impacting growth, so the obvious solution will be a more flexible exchange rate in the not-so-distant future," said Woolfolk.  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades in a narrow band linked to the dollar and is widely believed to be undervalued. Beijing has been under pressure from the United States and, increasingly, Europe, to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow on Thursday said China's interest rate hike was consistent with a move toward a flexible exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 But an official at the International Monetary Fund said the rate hike was "not necessarily" connected to any shift in exchange rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, Japan's top financial diplomat Hiroshi Watanabe said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in the past two weeks had been rapid and Japan would take decisive action if needed. Many traders believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen as the dollar falls closer to 105 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041028e0as008ss\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
