\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar eases as market discounts China rate move.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Ricci  \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2004\par 18:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 28 (Reuters) - The dollar eased on Thursday after a short-lived rally on news China had raised interest rates as the market decided Beijing's move did not radically change market fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar gyrated wildly in the aftermath of the move as the market pondered whether the tightening would adversely affect Asian economies and commodity-exporting countries, which would strengthen the dollar, or presaged a yuan revaluation, which would weaken the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 As the dust cleared, most economists decided that the rate hike would not push forward the timing of a revaluation - and could push it back - and that commodity exporters were likely to be hurt but not devastated by weaker demand.  \par \par \uc2 The impact of a slowing Chinese economy on the United States, which imports far more than it exports to China, also was likely to be small, some said.  \par \par \uc2 "Our thinking that a revaluation will come in the second half of next year is unchanged," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York, said he believed the rate hike "takes off the table" a near-term change in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg but helps set the stage for a revaluation in future.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a limit to how high they can raise rates without negatively impacting growth, so the obvious solution will be a more flexible exchange rate in the not-so-distant future," he said.  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades in a narrow band linked to the dollar and is widely believed to be undervalued. Beijing has been under pressure from the United States and, increasingly, Europe, to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs John Taylor on Thursday said China's interest rate hike was consistent with a move toward a flexible exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the benchmark one-year yuan lending rate to 5.58 percent from 5.31 percent, and the rate on one-year deposits to 2.25 percent from 1.98 percent in an effort to cool its red-hot economy.  \par \par \uc2 UP, THEN DOWN  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market's initial response to China's announcement was to buy back dollars that had been sold short in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 "There was a lot of conjecture about what it meant, which just created confusion. What that did was to purge a lot of speculative positions," said Grant Wilson, vice president at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar quickly turned weaker as traders refocused on the factors that have been weighing on it, namely mixed U.S. economic data, low U.S. interest rates, a growing current account gap and uncertainty ahead of Tuesday's presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 By midday in New York, the euro was up 0.31 percent at $1.2743 . The dollar was down a sharp 0.68 percent against the Swiss franc at 1.1983 francs .   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of commodity-exporting countries recovered smartly after\par earlier getting pounded. The Australian dollar was up about a third of\par a percent at 74.74 U.S. cents  while the U.S. dollar was off nearly a\par half percent versus the Canadian dollar at C$1.2198       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But dealers said they could come under pressure over time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Those \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been in the process of surging against the dollar and I think that surge will be cooled," said ABN Amro's Anderson.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the yen, the dollar was down 0.24 percent at 106.12 yen .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier, Japan's top financial diplomat, Hiroshi Watanabe, said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves in the past two weeks had been rapid and Japan would take decisive action if needed. Many traders believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would consider \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy dollars for yen as the dollar falls closer to 105 yen.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041028e0as007ho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
