\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan to take firm action on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  if needed-Watanabe.  \b0\par\par\uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2004\par 13:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat repeated his warning against a recent fall in the dollar against the yen, saying the ministry would take decisive action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed.  \par \par \uc2 His comments came after the dollar briefly dipped below 106 yen on Thursday as worries about the U.S. economy lingered despite a pullback in sky-high oil prices that helped it recover against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "We are watching the market with great interest... We are monitoring if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels are reflecting economic fundamentals and we will take action against movements that are disorderly and rapid," Hiroshi Watanabe, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been reacting to factors such as the impact of higher oil prices on the U.S. economy and uncertainties ahead of the U.S. presidential election, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in the past two weeks have been "rapid and volatile."  \par \par \uc2 "If needed, we will take decisive action in line with the spirit expressed in statements that have been issued by Group of Seven industrial nations (in the past)."  \par \par \uc2 He refrained from explaining specifically how he decides when the Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, only saying the ministry would make a "comprehensive judgement".  \par \par \uc2 "My answer is 'no comment' if you ask whether we are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market today," he added. He also declined to comment on certain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if Japan's better economic conditions compared to last year, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would make it harder for the ministry to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, Watanabe said, "Not particularly."  \par \par \uc2 He added that Japanese firms may not be much better off with a strong yen than last year and it would be needed to monitor economic situations at micro-levels as well when deciding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities spent a record 20 trillion yen ($187.6 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and another 15 trillion yen from January to March in an effort to curb yen strength, and have repeatedly said excessive and speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are undesirable.  \par \par \uc2 "I am doing my job with vigilance," Watanabe said. But he added that the ministry were not having "sleepless nights" to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements as some newspaper reported.  \par \par \uc2 Later on Thursday, the dollar spiked to session highs against the yen and the euro, following news that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its yuan interest rates. Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  implication of this move were unclear.  \par \par \uc2 At 1130 GMT the dollar was hovering near 106.70 yen .  \par \par \uc2 Asked his view on growing current account deficit in the United States, Watanabe said he did not think the situation is "suddenly worsening."  \par \par \uc2 "The United States can still attract capital from the world," Watanabe said. "I don't think the balance between surplus in the U.S. capital account and deficit in the current account has collapsed."  \par \par \uc2 He remained optimistic about the U.S. economic outlook, saying: "I have no worries about the United States continuing being the engine of growth (for the world)."  \par \par \uc2 ($1=106.63 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041028e0as005hq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
