\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar bounces off lows, but no respite seen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2004\par 03:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 26 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered just above multi-month lows on Tuesday as nagging worries about the strength of the U.S. economy and the gaping trade deficit kept investors on the back foot.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the dollar would likely stay under pressure from a range of factors, including uncertainty about the result of November's presidential election and comments from European officials that suggest they are comfortable with a higher euro.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think we've seen the end of this bout of dollar weakness," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase in Tokyo, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to gain support from data due this week.  \par \par \uc2 By 0053 GMT, the dollar bought around 106.82 yen, up around 0.1 percent on the day but not far off the six-month low of 106.22 it scraped on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, which has gained close to five percent in the past three weeks, was down around 0.2 percent at $1.2785. In U.S. trade it rose to a fresh eight-month high of 1.2830, just one cent below a record high of 1.2930 struck in February.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index which shows the dollar's movement against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the euro and the yen, hit an eight-month low on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The index has dipped 3.4 percent since the release of data on Oct. 14 that showed the U.S. trade deficit in August was the second-largest on record.  \par \par \uc2 An address by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet on Monday also helped to underpin the euro, though the market was more interested in what he did not say.  \par \par \uc2 In his annual report to the European Union parliament, Trichet did not speak directly on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That contrasted with his comments in January, when he called the euro's surge "brutal", sending it tumbling.  \par \par \uc2 Other ECB officials have said in recent days that a higher euro poses no problems.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said rising risk aversion in the run-up to the tightly contested U.S. presidential election on Nov. 2 was also hurting the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers say a victory for Democratic Sen. John Kerry over U.S. President George W. Bush would be a signal to sell the dollar since his administration would likely be less tolerant about any Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Still, in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is benefitting from strong exports to China, officials have shown less concern about a stronger yen than last year.  \par \par \uc2 In 2003, they spent a record 20 trillion yen to help prop up the dollar and defend the country's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan will take action if the dollar falls too quickly against the yen, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe said on Monday, helping the dollar temporarily pare some losses against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 But many believe that such action is unlikely in the near-term.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think we'll see any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the dollar/yen breaks below 103," said JPMorgan's Sasaki.  \par \par \uc2 Later on Tuesday the U.S. Conference Board will release October consumer confidence data. Economists in a Reuters survey expect a median reading of 94.0 compared with 96.8 in September.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041026e0aq0044m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
