\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits 8-month low vs euro, no respite seen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2004\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 26 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled to a fresh eight month low against the euro on Tuesday as nagging worries about the strength of the U.S. economy and the gaping trade deficit kept investors on the back foot.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the dollar would likely stay under pressure from a range of factors, including uncertainty about the result of November's presidential election and comments from European officials that suggest they are comfortable with a higher euro.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think we've seen the end of this bout of dollar weakness," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase in Tokyo, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to gain support from economic data due this week.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, which has gained close to five percent in the past three weeks, rose as high as $1.2839 . As of 0248 GMT it was at around 1.2835, less than one cent below a record high of 1.2930 struck in February.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar bought around 106.67 yen , barely changed on the day and not far off the six-month low of 106.22 it scraped on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc was at around 1.1930 per dollar, after hitting a new eight year high of 1.2927.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index , which shows the dollar's movement against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the euro and the yen, hit an eight-month low on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The index has dipped around 3.7 percent since the release of data on Oct. 14 that showed the U.S. trade deficit in August was the second-largest on record.  \par \par \uc2 An address by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet on Monday also helped to underpin the euro.  \par \par \uc2 In his annual report to the European Union parliament, Trichet did not speak directly on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That contrasted with his comments in January, when he called the euro's surge "brutal", sending it tumbling.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. RATE RISES TO END?  \par \par \uc2 A surge in oil prices to record highs of above $55 dollars a barrel this week has heightened worries about the strength of the U.S. economy, and led some to question whether a series of interest rate rises by the Federal Reserve is coming to an end.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it's now a 50/50 question of whether the Fed is still going to raise rates in November," said Hideaki Furumaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Trust & Custody Services Bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "If they decide to hold off it would show that the Fed has turned bearish on the U.S. economy, and obviously that would not be a good scenario for the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 The Fed has raised rates three times this year to 1.75 percent, and until very recently, was widely expected to continue that cycle when its policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) next meets on November 10, around a week after the U.S. presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 A further clue on the state of the economy will come later on Tuesday when the U.S. Conference Board releases October consumer confidence data. Economists in a Reuters survey expect a median reading of 94.0 compared with 96.8 in September.  \par \par \uc2 Some say a victory for Democratic Senator John Kerry over President George W. Bush in the election would be a signal to sell the dollar, since a Kerry administration would likely be less tolerant about any Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Still, in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is benefitting from strong exports to China, officials have shown less concern about a stronger yen than last year.  \par \par \uc2 In 2003, they spent a record 20 trillion yen to help prop up the dollar and defend the country's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan will take action if the dollar falls too quickly against the yen, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe said in New York on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 But many believe such action is unlikely in the near-term.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think we'll see any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the dollar/yen breaks below 103," said JP Morgan's Sasaki.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041026e0aq003q1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
