\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Falls To 8-Month Low Vs Euro; Flat Vs Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2004\par 07:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell to a fresh eight-month low against the euro but was barely changed against the yen in Asia Tuesday, as traders covered short dollar positions to take profits.  \par \par \uc2 The euro briefly broke above its overnight high to touch $1.2843, its highest level since Feb. 18, boosted by the euro/yen's rise on bids by Japanese players investing in euro-denominated securities, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 But the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lacked the momentum to head for the Feb. 18 all-time high of $1.2930, as many market players were eager to unwind long euro positions to book short-term profits at current levels, they said.  \par \par \uc2 Speculators' covering of dollar shorts also helped keep the greenback firmly supported above Y106.50, although market sentiment generally remained dollar-bearish, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Uncertainty over the U.S. presidential election is regarded as a risk factor for the dollar, but the weak-dollar trend won't change even after the election," said Masamichi Koike, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar may stage a modest rally ahead of the Nov. 2 presidential election as players adjust short positions, but a fall below Y106.00, or the euro/dollar's rise to a fresh record in the near term, "isn't out of the question," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High \par    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              106.69-73  106.75-81  -0.06  106.91  106.58   -0.61 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2825-27  1.2808-13  +0.13  1.2841  1.2774   +1.82 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.8430-40  1.8409-15  +0.11  1.8448  1.8394   +3.17 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.1941-45  1.1963-67  -0.18  1.1991  1.1927   -3.67 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.2200-03  1.2201-09  -0.01  1.2224  1.2186   -6.10 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.7486-91  0.7464-69  +0.29  0.7502  0.7472   -0.61 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.7000-07  0.6997-04  +0.04  0.7016  0.6994   +6.87 \par USD/DEM Deutsche Mark    2.2150-75  2.2150-75   0.00  2.2157  2.2160   -0.11 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 26-10-04 0530GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The mounting dollar-bearish mood has put a spotlight on the question of whether and when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will resume its dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent a sharp yen rise from derailing the nation's export-reliant economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some dollar bears believe the MOF won't be able to take action until the U.S. presidential election is over for fear of affecting its outcome.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities, says it's "too dangerous" to assume Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities won't act before or just after the election.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If it becomes absolutely necessary, the chance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (being conducted at such timing) can't be ruled out," Ueno said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In trying to gauge the timing of possible MOF action, many analysts point to levels such as Y106.12, the average assumed rate of big manufacturers for the latter half of this fiscal year shown in the Bank of Japan's "tankan" survey, and Y105.00, where many exporters have set their annual dollar/yen forecasts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A more important level, however, may be this year's low of Y103.40, which was set March 31 despite the massive yen-selling campaign the MOF was conducting at that time, analysts say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Unless the pace of the yen's rise becomes extremely fast, the MOF will resume its dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only when the greenback approaches Y103.40," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank of Tokyo.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In any case, the MOF is expected to examine various factors, such as overseas and domestic economic developments, as well as corporate earnings and stock price movements, in deciding when to take action, if any, analysts say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie [ 26-10-04 0651GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041026e0aq0006l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
