\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ Dollar Plunge Threatens To Slow Asian Growth, Rate Hikes  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Harui  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,278 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2004\par 09:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--After several months of relative stability, the dollar is plunging against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , threatening to slow the region's economic growth and delay Asian central banks' plans to raise interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The rise in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also increase pressure on China and, to a lesser extent, Malaysia to loosen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' pegs, letting the yuan and ringgit rise against the dollar. But Beijing and Kuala Lumpur have repeatedly made it clear they won't be rushed into liberalizing their fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes.  \par \par \uc2 'Markets can no longer deny that the dollar is again trending lower,' Lehman Brothers analysts said in a report. 'We do not think policymakers will aggressively resist dollar weakness this time, but it may take Asian policymakers some time to come around to that view.'  \par \par \uc2 Record-high oil prices and deep-seated worries over the U.S. trade and budget deficits, have knocked the dollar below monthslong ranges against many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the yen and the Singapore dollar hitting six-month highs Monday and the South Korean won notching a four-year peak against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar sank Monday to Y106.20, its lowest since April 13 and representing nearly a Y3 tumble in the past week, while falling to 1,136.3 won, its lowest since Nov. 15, 2000. Around 0925 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fetched Y106.34 and KRW1,134.9, while against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it was at S$1.6622, within striking distance of its April 13 low of S$1.6608.  \par \par \uc2 With economists already trimming their 2005 Asian economic growth forecasts, more expensive oil, slowing global demand for electronics and higher local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally spell bad news - many of the region's most vibrant economies are oil-consuming exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Sustained oil prices at current levels - crude futures hit another all-time high Monday, $55.58 a barrel, on the New York Mercantile Exchange - could slow economic growth in oil-dependent Asian nations by 1.5 to 2.0 percentage points next year, while an unexpectedly steep global tech slowdown could shave another 2 to 3 percentage points, said DBS Bank senior regional economist Chua Hak Bin.  \par \par \uc2 'The cumulative damage in such a bleak double-blow scenario could be as much as 3.5 to 5.0 percentage points,' depending on the nation, Chua said in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par               Easy On The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par   Others, however, say the outlook for Asia isn't so dire and, in fact, that the rise of the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shows the resilience of Asia-Pacific economies. \par   'The Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are aggressively higher because there is a shift in thinking about who the biggest losers are likely to be from high oil prices,' said a Singapore-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forwards trader. 'Yes, high oil prices will hurt Asia, but Asia is growing faster than Europe, so it's not the end of the world for the region.' \par   Some even see a silver lining - stronger local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  make it cheaper to import oil, which is denominated in dollars. That means the region's central banks may be less aggressive in selling their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for dollars than they were in the past, when fears over the health of their exporters were paramount. \par   The world's biggest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervener\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until this spring, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , hinted Monday it might be more tolerant of the yen's rise this time. Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves seem to reflect general dollar weakness, rather than yen strength. \par   Tanigaki reiterated, however, that Japan would monitor the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets closely, and his top bureaucrat said the government would step in if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  got out of line with economic fundamentals. His ministry sold a record Y35 trillion, almost all of it for dollars, in the 15 months through March to keep the strong yen from derailing Japan's export-reliant recovery and inflaming deflationary pressures. \par   China, by comparison, still buys huge amounts of dollars to keep the yuan pegged near 8.28 to the dollar. The U.S. and other major industrial powers have been pushing China for more than a year for greater \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility. Beijing says its ultimate goal is a fully convertible yuan but that it must take other steps first, such as shoring up its debt-laden banks. \par   But with other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  getting more expensive, the yuan peg means China exports are staying the same in dollar terms, making the contrast with many of its Asian competitors all the more glaring. The continued yuan weakness also thwarts China's efforts to slow its red-hot economy. \par   Nonetheless, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator warned this month that betting on a yuan rise is 'unwise and risky.' \par   In the offshore market for nondeliverable yuan forwards, a venue to bet on the fixed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar's discount to the yuan spot rate one year forward continued to steepen Monday, to 2690/2620 in late Asia trade. That implies expectations the yuan will rise 3.3% over the coming 12 months. As recently as late July, the NDFs market was pricing in just a 1.0% yuan rise over the subsequent 12 months. \par   Malaysia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had no comment on the rise in other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but market players said it's very unlikely for the authorities to react to the dollar's current fluctuations, given that Kuala Lumpur has stuck with its peg since September 1998, during the depths of the regional crisis. The wild card, however, is likely to be China. \par   'Malaysia has been pretty clear in laying out the issues, and China seems to be the key variable,' said Adam Le Mesurier, vice president of Asia-Pacific Economic Research at Goldman Sachs. 'If China changes its regime, it may prompt Malaysia to change its own.' \par  \par                  Easier To Be Easy \par  \par   Another result of stronger Asia-Pacific \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is that central banks may feel less compelled to raise interest rates to quell inflationary pressures. \par   The Reserve Bank of Australia, for one, could find the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market doing some of its work for it if the U.S. dollar continues to fall, said Richard Grace, a senior treasury economist at National Australia Bank. \par   A stronger Australian dollar would help the RBA in two ways - it would offset the mild inflationary pressure resulting from the Aussie's drop to a June low below 68 U.S. cents from February's seven-year high above 80 cents, and it would help counter the impact of higher oil prices on export price growth. The Aussie was at US$0.7485 late Monday. \par   By taking away some of the urgency to raise rates, a stronger local dollar 'provides a headwind for Australian growth,' Grace said. \par   Across the Tasman Sea, the Reserve Bank of New Zealand is widely expected to raise rates by 25 basis points Thursday to 6.50%. But partly because of the New Zealand dollar's rise - to one of its highest trade-weighted levels in the past 20 years - this could be the last hike of this cycle, said Deutsche Bank analysts. \par   'The rally in the New Zealand dollar over the past few weeks has been an important contributor to the market's changing assessment for the peak in the New Zealand cash rate,' the analysts said in a research report. \par  \par -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par    (Benjamin Pedley in Singapore, Hye-Seung Seo in Seoul, Jeff Meyer in Shanghai, Monica Houston-Waesch in Kuala Lumpur and Rebecca Thurlow in Sydney contributed to this article.) \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041026e0aq005k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
