\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits 8-month lows vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2004\par 14:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds euro zone comments, German data, Canadian dollar high)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 22 (Reuters) - The dollar hit 8-month lows against the euro and traded near multi-month lows against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday on worries about the U.S. economy and its ability to attract global investors.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen by 2-1/2 percent against the euro and the yen since Oct. 14, when the U.S. reported that it had recorded its second-largest monthly trade deficit in August.  \par \par \uc2 But several euro zone policymakers made relaxed comments about the euro's recent rise on Friday, adding to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upbeat tone.  \par \par \uc2 "We've had an accumulation of comments that a higher euro is not a problem," said Tony Norfield, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at ABN Amro.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose as far as $1.2658, but slipped to $1.2621 by 1209 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady against the yen at 107.50, from 4-month lows of 107.30 set on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 EURO ZONE TALK  \par \par \uc2 Bundesbank Vice President Juergen Stark said in an interview with Reuters he did not expect the current level of the euro to pose a lasting threat to Germany's economy.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Vitor Constancio said the rising euro was helping soften the pain of higher oil prices, and ECB Executive Board member Jose Manuel Gonzalez-Paramo said he was "not at all" concerned about the euro/dollar exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, the authorities in Tokyo are beginning to make warning noises about the yen's rise, which could hurt Japan's exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday his ministry would take action if the yen's value deviated from economic fundamentals.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since mid-March, when the yen was probing four-year highs against the dollar around 103.50 yen. Many traders think Tokyo could resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the dollar fall to around 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 ELECTION JITTERS  \par \par \uc2 High oil prices have likely pushed up German inflation in October by more than expected, according to data released on Friday from the states of Baden-Wuerttemberg and Saxony.  \par \par \uc2 However, there are no major U.S. data releases on Friday, after data in the previous session showing the Philly Fed index jumped in October to 28.5 from 13.4 in September, well above forecast.  \par \par \uc2 President George W. Bush has a slim one-point lead over Democratic rival John Kerry ahead of the Nov. 2 election, according to the latest Reuters/Zogby poll.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say Kerry is seen as more protectionist than Bush, and happy to see a weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Markets will be inclined to decrease their activities ahead of the impending U.S. election," said Armin Mekelburg, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich.  \par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Labour Statistics releases a state-by-state breakdown of September jobs data at 1400 GMT, which analysts said will show the impact of the hurricanes in Florida on the modest 96,000 gain in non-farm payrolls last month.  \par \par \uc2 CANADA, UK  \par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar hit its highest levels against the U.S. dollar since Nov. 1992, breaking the 1.24 per dollar barrier after the Bank of Canada's Monetary Policy Report said the economy was nearing full capacity and signalled more rate hikes were ahead to prevent overheating.  \par \par \uc2 High oil and commodity prices have also been helping the Canadian dollar in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling fell sharply after data on Friday showing third quarter British growth at a lower than expected 0.4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was trading at $1.8245, three quarters of a cent below 2-month highs set earlier in the session.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at 69.15 pence per euro, within half a penny of 9-month lows set earlier this week on ebbing expectations for further British rate hikes.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041022e0am00919\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
