\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar near 4-month lows, Tanigaki serves warning.  \b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2004\par 03:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 22 (Reuters) - The dollar was tracking near four-month lows against the yen on Friday after positive economic data failed to relieve investor worries about the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to an eight-month low against the euro and its lowest level against the yen since late June despite a better than expected rise in a regional manufacturing index and a drop in weekly jobless claims.  \par \par \uc2 "Yesterday's numbers were good but the dollar did not rebound. It tells you how bearish the market is on the dollar now," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 "I wasn't expecting the dollar to fall under 107 yen until after the U.S. election but after seeing yesterday's moves, I think that's possible."  \par \par \uc2 At 0100 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 107.59/64 yen compared with a four-month low of 107.35 on Thursday and 107.48 in late U.S. trade.  \par \par \uc2 There was little reaction to Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki's comment that his ministry would take any necessary action if the yen's value deviated from economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "I decline to comment on daily movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but, as we said in the G7 statement, it is a problem if \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  do not reflect economic fundamentals," Tanigaki told a regular news conference in response to a question about the yen's recent rise.  \par \par \uc2 "We need to carefully watch the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement ... As we always said, we will take necessary action if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements deviate from economic fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 SUPPORT AT 107 YEN  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in mid-March when the yen was probing four-year highs against the dollar around 103.50 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Worried about the impact of a strong yen on exports, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  orchestrated 15 trillion yen ($139.7 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first three months of the year after a record 20 trillion yen in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 For the time being, traders said the dollar was likely to hold above a key technical support level of 107 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "We are approaching the lower end of the 107-112 yen range and the push (down) from here will be tough," said Katsunori Kitakura, manager of Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking's treasury department.  \par \par \uc2 "Also ahead of the U.S. election, I don't think players will be eager to take large positions."  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2607/10 versus $1.2616.  \par \par \uc2 It rose as high as $1.2652 on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. government reported a drop in claims for jobless benefits to 329,000 in the latest week from 354,000 a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The Philadelphia Federal Reserve's business activity index also jumped in October to 28.5 from 13.4 in September.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts had been looking for a reading of 17.0.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also showed muted reaction to comments from Federal Reserve Bank Governor Ben Bernanke who reiterated the Fed will continue to raise interest rates at a "measured" pace.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders said the market could focus on remarks from San Francisco Federal Reserve President Janet Yellen, who said the Fed may need to modify its rate plan, depending on how economic activity and inflation fare.  \par \par \uc2 Yellen also reiterated her concerns that the dollar has remained "relatively high despite our large and growing trade deficit."  \par \par \uc2 By making imports cheaper, this undermined demand for domestic goods, and therefore interest rates were lower than they would otherwise be to support economic activity, Yellen said. ($1=107.41 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041022e0am005rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
