\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rebounds from lows but still vulnerable.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2004\par 16:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates prices, adds comment and quotes, changes byline and dateline. Previous: LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 22 (Reuters) - The dollar was a bit stronger early in New York on Friday, not on any positive fundamental developments, but benefiting from a technical rebound after briefly hitting fresh lows earlier in the session.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's backdrop remains negative, with traders citing mixed U.S. economic data, record external deficits, waning capital inflows and a generally deteriorating technical picture as reasons to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But as gloomy sentiment and price declines reach greater extremes, so the dollar is prone to corrective bounces as dealers temporarily buy it back to cover "short" positions, or bets it will weaken further.  \par \par \uc2 "Let's face it, if you're buying euros in the $1.23s and $1.24s, you've had a nice run," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at ING Capital Markets in New York. "Somebody just came in and took some money off the table," he said, referring to the euro's sharp pullback earlier Friday from $1.2658, a fresh eight-month high.  \par \par \uc2 At 9:40 a.m. EDT (1330 GMT), the euro was trading around $1.2614 , flat from late New York on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was off its lows against the yen and Swiss franc, up a bit\par more at 107.60 yen  and 1.2195 francs       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling was off a bit at $1.8241 , under pressure for most of the global session after data showed that the UK economy grew by 0.4 percent in the third quarter compared with the previous three months. Economists had expected 0.5 percent growth. Even that would have been sharply down from the 0.9 percent growth recorded in the second quarter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On a trade-weighted basis, the dollar briefly touched a fresh eight-month low Friday of 85.87 before recovering to 86.10.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SOUND OF SILENCE  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's downturn in recent weeks has been sign-posted by U.S. jobs, trade and capital flows data as well as Federal Reserve commentary on the unsustainability of the U.S. current account. The latest piece of rhetoric came from San Francisco Fed President Janet Yellen, who said on Thursday the dollar is still "relatively high despite our large and growing trade deficit."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's slide has fueled speculation euro-zone authorities will step in - verbally or otherwise - to stem the appreciation of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But European officials, from finance ministers to European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council members, have said the euro's strength poses no problems at all.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We haven't seen anything from the Europeans on the strength of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Michael McGuinness, senior director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at American Express in New York. "That seems to be a green light to test the (euro's) upside."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger euro makes euro-zone exports more expensive on global markets, which cuts into the region's economic growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In contrast, authorities in Tokyo are beginning to make warning noises about the yen's rise, which could hurt Japan's exporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday his ministry would take action if the yen's value deviated from economic fundamentals.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since mid-March, when the yen was probing four-year highs against the dollar around 103.50 yen. Many traders think Tokyo could resume its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should the dollar fall to around 105 yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the dollar slumped to a new 12-year low against the Canadian dollar, dipping below C$1.24 briefly before recovering to C$1.2429.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041023e0am002by\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
