\par\par \b \uc2 DATA VIEW: Japan Data Shows Soft Econ, Yen Worries Govt  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi and Miyako Takebe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2004\par 04:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese economic activity rebounded by only 0.2% in August after a 0.6% fall in July, government data showed Friday, in the latest sign that the pace of the economy's recovery is slowing.  \par \par \uc2 The seasonally adjusted 0.2% rise in the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry's all industry index - a supply-side measure of economic growth - came in just short of economists' forecasts, which had centered on a 0.3% rise.  \par \par \uc2 The figures highlight a sluggishness in the world's second-largest economy that won't be helped by a renewed fall in the dollar, which has slid to a three-month low against the yen, worrying Japanese government officials.  \par \par \uc2 "As it says in the (Group of Seven) statement, it is a problem when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates don't reflect fundamentals," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told reporters, referring to recent communiques by G7 finance ministers.  \par \par \uc2 "If the market doesn't reflect fundamentals and moves in a speculative fashion, we will take the necessary action," he said.  \par \par \uc2 A stronger yen threatens Japanese exports, which were the initial driving force behind the country's economic recovery but look less able to support the economy as growth slows in the U.S. and China, Japan's biggest overseas markets.  \par \par \uc2 METI's all industry index, which stood at 102.1 in August, has jumped around in recent months, falling 0.6% in July, rising 0.7% rise in June and dropping 0.8% drop in May.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts say that, overall, all industry activity is running below its level in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The index must rise more than 0.9% in September in order for it to post a rise for the third quarter, said Satoru Ogasawara, an economist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "The economy is obviously moderating from very strong growth" in the last quarter of 2003 and the first quarter of 2004, he said. "We are forecasting this correction phase will continue to the end of this fiscal year," which ends in March 2005.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Typhoon Disrupts Economy \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 METI's tertiary industry index, a gauge of strength in the services sector, rose 0.4% in August from the previous month to a seasonally adjusted 104.1 after falling 0.8% in July.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The increase in the tertiary index was near to economists' forecasts, but also is running at a pace below that in the previous quarter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tertiary index reflects activity in 11 industries, including utilities, real estate, transport, telecommunications, wholesale and retail, and finance and insurance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rise in August was due mostly to a rebound in activity in the information technology sector, which rose 7.8%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Nonetheless, the sluggishness of this index is rather disappointing, as nonmanufacturing activity looks to be taking a breather along with industrial production," said Ryo Hino, an economist at JPMorgan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 METI maintained its upbeat view on the data series, noting that in on-year terms, both the all-industry and tertiary activity indexes were up for the 12th straight month in August.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The results "show that activity stayed on a gradual uptrend that has continued since late last year," a METI official said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August's gain in the overall index was modest partly because typhoons caused disruption to transportation and discouraged people from traveling and shopping, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka was less upbeat and told reporters that the government needs to ascertain what impact the latest typhoon has had on the economy. Typhoon Tokage, the deadliest in more than a decade, swept across Japan this week, leaving at least 63 people dead and 25 missing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With all signs pointing to softer economic growth in Japan, the last thing the country needs is a stronger yen to choke off its exports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan spent Y35 trillion between January 2003 and March 2004 to stop the dollar's fall and market participants speculate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will order the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to resume its yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the dollar continues to weaken.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0145 GMT, the dollar was at Y107.70, up from Y107.42 in late New York trading Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's former vice finance minister for international affairs, Eisuke Sakakibara, told Dow Jones Newswires Thursday that the MOF would likely wait until the dollar hit Y100 before \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi and Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (James Simms and Iain McDonald contributed to this article.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 22-10-04 0229GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041022e0am0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
