\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki: Watching  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Moves Carefully  \b0\par\par\uc2 190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2004\par 02:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to take action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates move in a way that doesn't reflect economic fundamentals, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "As it says in the (Group of Seven) statement, it is a problem when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates don't reflect fundamentals," Tanigaki told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "We will continue to closely monitor (the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets)," although he added that he wouldn't comment on individual market moves.  \par \par \uc2 "If the market doesn't reflect fundamentals and move in a speculative fashion, we will take the necessary action."  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki also declined to comment on speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may find it harder to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the time of the U.S. presidential elections Nov. 2.  \par \par \uc2 At 0054 GMT, the dollar was changing hands at Y107.55, edging back up from Y107.42 overnight in New York, though still well down from the Y109s just two days earlier.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson and Shawn Schroter [ 22-10-04 0059GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041022e0am0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
