\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar struggles under weight of funding fears.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2004\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates prices, adds comments and quotes, changes byline and dateline. Previous: LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 21 (Reuters) - The dollar was weaker across the board early in New York on Thursday, forging new lows on a growing sense that the United States is struggling to fund its record external deficits.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's break of key technical chart support levels in recent sessions follows sluggish economic and capital flows data, and a batch of Federal Reserve comments on the unsustainability of the U.S. current account deficit at present levels.  \par \par \uc2 The starkest came on Oct. 7, when Dallas Fed President Robert McTeer said: "Over time, there's only one direction for the dollar to go - lower."  \par \par \uc2 "That gave the green light to all of this," said Craig Russell, senior trader at Alaron \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 Four Fed officials, governors Ben Bernanke and Susan Bies, and St. Louis and San Francisco Fed Presidents William Poole and Janet Yellen, are scheduled to speak later on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness has intensified and its general backdrop has deteriorated, as evidenced by falling bond yields and equities and rising oil and gold. Indeed, the yield on the 10-year Treasury note dipped to a six-month low below 3.96 percent a short while ago Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Lower yields weigh on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because investors look to fixed income assets in other countries for higher returns.  \par \par \uc2 "Extensive dollar selling looks set to continue, given the lack of fundamental reasons for a reversal," wrote JP Morgan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts in a research note on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Any bounce in the dollar is likely to be muted," noted their counterparts at HSBC.  \par \par \uc2 That appears to be borne out by the dollar's reaction to the latest snapshot of the U.S. labor market. Jobless claims fell by 25,000 to 329,000 from a revised 354,000 the previous week, the Labor Department said on Thursday. Economists had expected a fall of 7,000. But the dollar's lift was negligible.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems like a pretty solid number," said Lara Rhame, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CSFB in New York. "(But) this positive surprise was not enough to offer support to the dollar initially and looking ahead, the Philly Fed number is what markets are truly focusing on."  \par \par \uc2 The Philadelphia Federal Reserve's gauge of manufacturing activity for October in the U.S. Mid-Atlantic region will be released at 12:00 p.m. EDT (1600 GMT). Economists' forecasts for the business activity index ranged from a reading of 9.0 to 23.0, compared with 13.4 in September. The median was 17.0.  \par \par \uc2 Early in New York on Thursday, the euro was stronger at $1.2607 . Earlier in the day it traded at $1.2651, its strongest since late February.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker at 1.2182 Swiss francs , having hit an eight-year low earlier in the session of 1.2150 francs, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 It was also off at 107.60 yen . Earlier, it hit a four-month low of 107.47 yen.  \par \par \uc2 As the dollar sinks against the yen, speculation of either verbal or actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities rises.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't sold yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since mid-March, but vice finance minister Koichi Hosokawa said his ministry would continue monitoring moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  carefully and take appropriate action if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was stronger after a bigger-than-expected jump in U.K. retails sales, trading up at $1.8270 .  \par \par \uc2 High-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian and New Zealand dollars also scored multi-month records against the greenback. The Canadian dollar also held near its recent 11-1/2 year high.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by John Parry).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041022e0al005qt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
