\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Midday  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Rebounds Slightly From Early Slide  \b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2004\par 22:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 21, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is rebounding slightly from its lows at midday in Europe Thursday but remains depressed by continued heavy investment flows working against it. Concerns about the difficulty the U.S. will have funding its current account deficit were made even worse by an overnight decline in U.S. Treasury yields, with the 10-year benchmark falling back under 4% to 3.97%. As a result, some market sources reported that the only foreign buyer of U.S. government bonds is the Bank of Japan, which is managing its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in its usual manner. Otherwise, the dollar is attracting little support as the price if crude oil resumes its recent rally and futures prices rise close to $55 a barrel. Near midday, the price of the December contract is at $54.62, down from an earlier high at $54.95 but still well above the $54.40 level quoted late in Asian trading. At one stage, the dollar was sold as low as Y107.48 but rebounded to Y107.55 as the market became concerned about the risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This compared with Y108.21 late in New York Wednesday. For the second consecutive day the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was said to have been checking quotes in the market. Vice-finance minister Koichi Hosakawa warned the authorities will take appropriate action if the yen deviates from economic fundamentals. Given the continued reliance of the Japanese recovery on export growth, the authorities are known to be keen to prevent a rise in the yen that might make Japanese goods less competitive in world markets. The yen's advance was also expected to be limited by the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data from showing that, while net investment flows turned positive for Japan last week, foreign interest in Japanese equities has taken a dive. Foreigners sold Y34 billion in stocks last week after three consecutive weeks of steadily increasing equity purchases. Given the continued rise in oil prices, this trend is likely to continue, said Ashley Davies, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS in Singapore. "Coupled with the lack of interest in Japanese equities we think that dollar weakness is best played against euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than the yen," he said. The euro, meanwhile, rose as far as $1.2650 at one stage during the morning before falling back to trade at $1.2624 at midday, up from $1.2587 late Wednesday in New York. Buyers of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were encouraged by signs that euro zone politicians are becoming increasingly tolerant of a strong euro. Nicholas Sarkozy, France's outgoing finance minister, told reporters late Wednesday that it is much better to have a strong euro rather than a weak euro when oil prices are heading higher. Other finance ministers attending a meeting in Luxembourg echoed these sentiments, with Luxembourg's Jean Claude Juncker suggesting that the euro's recent rise isn't of great concern. Austria's Karl Grasser said the current level of the euro won't hurt exports. Sterling was the other big mover of the morning, rising to nearly $1.8300 after the U.K. released data suggesting British consumers may still be more active than the market expected. Retail sales last month rose by 1.0%. This was well above the 0.2% increase that had been forecast and the 0.7% rise registered in August. However, market analysts suggested that this number wouldn't be enough to change policy on its own and the Bank of England is still unlikely to raise interest rates further at this stage. The pound is up at $1.8293 from $1.8172 while the dollar has also fallen to CHF1.2168 from CHF1.2223.  \par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041023e0al000a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
