\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Sakakibara: Dollar Likely To Head For Y100  \b0\par\par\uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2004\par 10:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar will likely head for Y100 in the near-term as the market worries about negative surprises that might hit the U.S. economy, a former senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. economy seems highly vulnerable to a negative shock," and U.S. officials appear overly optimistic about the prospect for economic growth and of the impact of higher crude oil prices, Eisuke Sakakibara, a former Japan vice finance minister for international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara, who was nicknamed "Mr. Yen" because of the aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigns he conducted in the late 1990's while at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was speaking after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell Thursday to as low as Y107.77, its lowest level since July 12.  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara's remarks represent a change in his views regarding the outlook for the yen.  \par \par \uc2 In July, Sakakibara had said he thought the dollar would rise toward Y120 by year-end, rather than fall, on the possibility that Japan's economy would start to deteriorate and thus lead to a weaker yen.  \par \par \uc2 But he changed his forecast after making a visit to the U.S. recently, during which he met with both current and former government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials in Washington and New York.  \par \par \uc2 Not only did many of those he met seem overly optimistic about the U.S. economy and about the impact of higher crude oil prices, a "surprisingly large" number seemed to hold the view that "some sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment," could be used to address the U.S. twin current account and budget deficits, said Sakakibara, who is now a professor at Japan's Keio University.  \par \par \uc2 "It seemed to me that the U.S. could be hurt more by negative surprises than Japan could."  \par \par \uc2 If and when the U.S. does get hurt, Washington may choose to tolerate a dollar fall in a form of "benign neglect," even without explicitly saying that's what it's doing, Sakakibara said.  \par \par \uc2 Such benign neglect could come in the form of increased official commentary on how the current account deficit was becoming unsustainably large, or from a lack of reference to a strong dollar unless forced, Sakakibara said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  May Wait A Bit Before \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara also said that while Japanese authorities will no doubt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the dollar's fall if it becomes too sharp, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe may choose to wait until the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hits Y100 before acting.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities have a precise grasp of market positioning, and based on such knowledge, Watanabe - who took over from Zembei Mizoguchi in July - "will likely think of ways to surprise the market," Sakakibara said. "Watanabe is a master of mystery."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In any case, "it won't be like before, when the MOF could be counted on to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and satisfy the market's expectations for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From January 2003, when Mizoguchi took office, until March this year, the MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  massively and so frequently that its actions rarely took traders by surprise. The MOF spent a record Y35 trillion selling yen, mostly for dollars, during that period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Watanabe has a choice that Mizoguchi didn't have - to not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for a bit," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "He will likely be flexible in considering a lot of choices - such as letting the dollar fall to Y90 before stepping in" the former bureaucrat said, although he didn't rule out the possibility the ministry may act above Y100 as well.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara also said that the MOF's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tactics may change to reflect the fact that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy has recovered considerably since the MOF was selling yen to protect the recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nov. 2 U.S. presidential election may make it harder for Japan to act as well, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Since we have to gain the understanding of the other party (the U.S.) when we \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it will be more difficult to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  than at other times," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It may be particularly difficult this year as some of the key election battles are taking place in states like Michigan and Ohio where manufacturing employees are a key constituency and dislike Tokyo's policy of weakening the yen to make Japanese goods cheap overseas, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If John Kerry is elected, it may be more difficult to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the Democratic Senator is more likely than incumbent George W. Bush to frown upon \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , unless it's absolutely necessary, Sakakibara said. It would also be harder to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during a new administration's transition period, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moreover, Robert Rubin, one of Kerry's economic advisers and a Treasury Secretary during the Clinton administration, "has never really liked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  either," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara concluded that in any case, he is looking forward to seeing how and when Watanabe "debuts" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I had my own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  style. Mizoguchi had his style, and Watanabe will have his."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie and Hugh Lawson [ 21-10-04 0813GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041021e0al000cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
