l prices, a "surprisingly large" number seemed to hold the view that "some sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment," could be used to address the U.S. twin current account and budget deficits, said Sakakibara, who is now a professor at Japan's Keio University.  \par \par \uc2 "It seemed to me that the U.S. could be hurt more by negative surprises than Japan could."  \par \par \uc2 If and when the U.S. does get hurt, Washington may choose to tolerate a dollar fall in a form of "benign neglect," even without explicitly saying that's what it's doing, Sakakibara said.  \par \par \uc2 Such benign neglect could come in the form of increased official commentary on how the current account deficit was becoming unsustainably large, or from a lack of reference to a strong dollar unless forced, Sakakibara said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  May Wait A Bit Before \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara also said that while Japanese authorities will no doubt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the dollar's fall if it becomes too sharp, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe may choose to wait until the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hits Y100 before acting.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities have a precise grasp of market positioning, and based on such knowledge, Watanabe - who took over from Zembei Mizoguchi in July - "will likely think of ways to surprise the market," Sakakibara said. "Watanabe is a master of mystery."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In any case, "it won't be like before, when the MOF could be counted on to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and satisfy the market's expectations for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From January 2003, when Mizoguchi took office, until March this year, the MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  massively and so frequently that its actions rarely took traders by surprise. The MOF spent a record Y35 trillion selling yen, mostly for dollars, during that period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Watanabe has a choice that Mizoguchi didn't have - to not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for a bit," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "He will likely be flexible in considering a lot of choices - such as letting the dollar fall to Y90 before stepping in" the former bureaucrat said, although he didn't rule out the possibility the ministry may act above Y100 as well.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara also said that the MOF's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tactics may change to reflect the fact that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy has recovered considerably since the MOF was selling yen to protect the recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nov. 2 U.S. presidential election may make it harder for Japan to act as well, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Since we have to gain the understanding of the other party (the U.S.) when we \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it will be more difficult to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  than at other times," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It may be particularly difficult this year as some of the key election battles are taking place in states like Michigan and Ohio where manufacturing employees are a key constituency and dislike Tokyo's policy of weakening the yen to make Japanese goods cheap overseas, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If John Kerry is elected, it may be more difficult to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the Democratic Senator is more likely than incumbent George W. Bush to frown upon \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , unless it's absolutely necessary, Sakakibara said. It would also be harder to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during a new administration's transition period, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moreover, Robert Rubin, one of Kerry's economic advisers and a Treasury Secretary during the Clinton administration, "has never really liked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  either," Sakakibara said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara concluded that in any case, he is looking forward to seeing how and when Watanabe "debuts" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I had my own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  style. Mizoguchi had his style, and Watanabe will have his."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie and Hugh Lawson [ 21-10-04 0805GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041021e0al00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
