\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Reaches Fresh Low Vs Euro After TICS Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Laurence Norman  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2004\par 16:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar was pushed lower across the board Monday morning in New York after the U.S. Treasury announced that foreign investors' net purchases of U.S. securities in August declined from the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 After remaining steady in overnight trading against the euro and gaining against the yen, the dollar got a fresh blow from the data on Treasury International Capital System flows. Net foreign buys of U.S. securities in August were $59.0 billion, lower than July's downwardly revised net purchases of $63.1 billion.  \par \par \uc2 In response to the release of the TICS data, the dollar sank to an almost eight-month low against the euro and fell to a fresh three-month low against the Swiss franc. The dollar had reached a seven-month low against the euro Friday after U.S. economic data suggested the fourth quarter had made a slow start.  \par \par \uc2 The data added to already deep concerns about the sustainability of the huge U.S. trade and current account deficits. With foreigners buying fewer U.S. Treasuries, stocks and other dollar assets, the large trade deficits will place strong downward pressure on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At 10.30 a.m. EDT (1430 GMT), the euro was at $1.2519 from $1.2476 in New York Friday. That tops Friday's high of $1.2505 and gives more indication of a decisive move out of the multimonth range against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was fetching Y109.18, little changed from Y109.17 in late New York trading Friday. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank against the Swiss franc, trading at a three-month low of CHF1.2297 from CHF1.234 Friday. The pound was at $1.8017 from $1.8036 Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Andy Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Harris Nesbitt Burns, said that even though the market was in the mood to seize on any negative data and drive the dollar lower, the August TICS data was unquestionably bad for the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 "It's really come off the last couple of months. We're in dangerous territory. .. The U.S. has a big problem with its trade balances," he said. A weaker dollar "is the only way we can partially address the trade data."  \par \par \uc2 Busch said the next key resistance point for the euro was around $1.2540. Once it pierces that level, he said the dolalr could go all the way up to $1.2630.  \par \par \uc2 He said the yen may gain further against the dollar and that could set up the scenario of earlier 2004 when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan interveneed\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to stem the dollar's slide. In addition to the TICS report, the U.S. will release its October housing market index at 1 p.m. EDT. Traders are also watching for the release of the Sep consumer price index, which will come Tuesday morning.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there was little reaction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market overnight to news that the euro-zone countries' September consumer price index came in at 2.1%, from 2.3% in August. The data was in line with expectations.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling, however, fell back in early New York trading on the back of negative data, including a fall in September consumer confidence and a decline in housing prices.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the yen got some support overnight from positive comments by Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui, who told \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  branch managers that "the nation's economy continues to recover."  \par \par \uc2 However, any positive spinoff from those comments was checked by another record high crude price in overnight trading, but has now fallen to $54.69 per barrel. Nymex light, sweet crude for November delivery reached $53.33 a barrel. That helped send Tokyo stocks lower for a seventh straight session.  \par \par \uc2 -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 1-201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com [ 10-18-04 1035ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1074 1863 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041018e0ai00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
