\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:US May Be Returning To 80's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy -Analyst  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Miyako Takebe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2004\par 07:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar could fall sharply below Y100 over the next year if the U.S. economy slows and the current account deficit widens, as a growing number of U.S. officials seem to be viewing a weaker dollar as a possible policy option, an analyst at Japan's Keio University says.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a risk of dollar depreciation over the course of the next year...(because) in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, many in the U.S. seem to be going back to the '80s," said Toru Umemoto, financial market analyst at Keio's Global Security Research Center.  \par \par \uc2 In the 1980's, governments often regarded \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates as a means to adjust trade imbalances, unlike now, when most governments prefer to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to market forces.  \par \par \uc2 Umemoto, who works under Eisuke Sakakibara, a former vice finance minister for international affairs, said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires that he had recently visited the U.S. and met with both current and former government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials in Washington and New York.  \par \par \uc2 "Many (officials) were concerned over the current account deficit, which they said is a problem as it is too large. They pointed out that 'to a certain degree' a weaker dollar could be effective" in reducing it, Umemoto said.  \par \par \uc2 He added that many officials were upbeat about the prospects for the U.S. economy and were also optimistic in the sense that they believe crude oil prices won't affect the economy too much.  \par \par \uc2 They also expected China to shift to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime within the next few years, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Taken together, Umemoto "was left with an impression" that if higher crude oil prices wind up having an unexpectedly big impact on the U.S. economy and they force a change in the upbeat outlook, the issue of the current account deficit and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - including China's yuan - will likely return to the forefront of political debate, he said.  \par \par \uc2 If that happens, U.S. officials might tolerate a dollar fall in a "benign neglect" manner, without explicitly saying that they do, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Given that financial markets are sensitive to any real or perceived shifts in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, a lack of action by the U.S. if the dollar falls sharply could, "in my view, send the dollar down to around Y90," said Umemoto, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \uc2 "Of course, in the unlikely event that the U.S. decides to openly express a desire for a weaker dollar, we could see Y70." Around 0500 GMT Thursday, the dollar was trading around Y109.70.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        Weak Dollar Failed To Shrink Deficit In Past \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. current account deficit, which now totals around 5.7% of the U.S. gross domestic product, could top the 6% mark next year, some economists say, as higher crude oil prices push up the U.S. net import bill while a lack of global demand keeps U.S. exports weak.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But history has shown a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ineffective in reducing the deficit, Umemoto said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the early to mid-1990s, the dollar weakened considerably when the administration of U.S. President Bill Clinton came under pressure from domestic industries and focused on trade issues with Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not having a clear \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy at the time, the Clinton administration's solution to fixing Japan's massive current account surplus and the gaping U.S. current account deficit was to tinker with exchange rates, observers say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But even as the dollar weakened, the current account deficit didn't decline," Umemoto said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Eventually, the dollar's slide got out of hand, requiring concerted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hoist the greenback off an all-time low of Y79.80 marked in April 1995.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since then, U.S. officials have become extremely careful in resorting to a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for political trade gains. In the end, the U.S. settled on a strong dollar policy, which helped finance the current account deficit, Umemoto said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He stresses that a dollar decline is only "one risk scenario," and not something he believes will necessarily happen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In any case, if the U.S. unit does start to slide, Japanese officials will likely slow the fall by resuming \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Umemoto said.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stayed out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for six months after ending in mid-March a 15-month \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spree during which it sold a total of Y35 trillion, mostly for dollars, to keep a strong yen from snuffing out an economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But "with Japan still in deflation, a strong yen would obviously not be welcome," Umemoto said, adding that the focus would be on when and how Tokyo chooses to slow the dollar's fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie [ 14-10-04 0544GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041014e0ae0005d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
