\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Hosoda: No Need To Change  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2004\par 04:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Nikkei/Dow Jones)--Japanese Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroyuki Hosoda said Thursday there is no need for Tokyo to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 The top government spokesman was speaking before Friday's meeting in Washington of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers.  \par \par \uc2 He added that although major U.S. automakers have complained repeatedly to U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, Tokyo hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in more than six months.  \par \par \uc2 Hosoda's comments follow market rumors earlier in the week that the Bank of Japan had bought dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Some market participants cited rumors the BOJ, acting under orders from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold yen for dollars overnight Tuesday - possibly to help alleviate the impact of higher crude oil prices on Japan's economy.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF will release monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market data for September at 1000 GMT Thursday, which will likely show whether Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last month. [ 30-09-04 0328GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040930e09u0002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
