\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drops ahead of G7, yen stabilises.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2004\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a two-month low against the euro and a one-month low against the Swiss franc on Thursday as investors sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of this Friday's meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers.  \par \par \uc2 The yen recovered its poise as oil prices eased off this week's record highs and investors looked ahead to a key survey of Japanese corporate confidence.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's meeting of G7 financial leaders in Washington will also be closely watched with investors hungry for comments on the global economic outlook and oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The euro's move was bolstered by options-related activity and nervousness that the forthcoming G7 meeting may call for further greenback depreciation, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a technical move and opens the way to further upside for the euro. There is also a feeling it is better to be short dollars ahead of G7," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior currrency strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "The speculative part of the market wants to push the euro higher," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose more than 0.7 percent against the greenback to climb above $1.2400 for the first time since mid-July. The dollar also fell almost one percent to 1.2488 Swiss francs in a matter of minutes.  \par \par \uc2 G7 TALKS  \par \par \uc2 A year ago G7 nations called for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to iron out imbalances in the global economy. The dollar fell sharply on the statement which was interpreted as a sign the United States wanted a weaker dollar to help reduce its trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the G7 to signal this time around that patience is needed in future monetary tightening around the world. U.S. interest rates have risen three times since June but recent weak data has raised doubts about the future pace of rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 A German government source said Germany and Britain would present the meeting with an initiative to boost oil market transparency to prevent speculation in the market from inflating the costs of crude.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices dipped further from recent peaks above $50 a barrel, helping Tokyo's Nikkei stock market average snap a nine-day slide and lifting the yen off four-month lows against the euro and six-week lows against the dollar hit earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Japan relies on imports for all its oil needs and the recent surge in energy costs has weighed heavily on both its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its stock market.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen came under the cosh earlier this week because of higher oil prices and so the slight retreat has given the yen and the Nikkei a boost," said Paul Robson, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Canada.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was down 0.4 percent at 110.40 yen while the euro was up 0.2 percent at 137.00 yen.  \par \par \uc2 DATA WATCH  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey, due on Friday, is expected to show corporate sentiment at its highest in more than a decade but also to signal a less robust outlook for the future.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. personal income and consumption figures for August are due at 1230 GMT ahead of a survey of business activity in the U.S. Midwest at 1400 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Data out of the euro zone was mixed.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone inflation subsided towards the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal in September even as consumer and business confidence rose tentatively.  \par \par \uc2 Prices paid by consumers rose 2.2 percent from a year earlier in September, a month in which oil prices hit record highs, after rising at a 2.3 percent rate in August.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry said it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign exchanges in September, staying out of the market for a sixth straight month after spending a record amount last year to stem the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Japan last stepped into the market on March 16 when the dollar fell almost two yen on the day to around 108.60. Since then, the yen has traded in a range between 103.40 per dollar - a four-year high - at the end of March to nearly 115 yen in mid-May.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top government spokesman, Hiroyuki Hosoda, said earlier that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were now moving in a stable manner and there was no need to change Tokyo's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040930e09u009kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
47
