\par\par \b \uc2 Japan seen taking back seat in G7 yuan debate.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ritsuko Ando   \par \uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2004\par 14:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - When the United States demands at this week's Group of Seven meeting that China loosen its grip on the yuan, Japan is expected to say little and try to avoid being drawn into the debate between its two biggest trading partners.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials want to give China more time to decide how and when to change \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and analysts say they are likely to look on as Washington, the host of the meeting, presses for a review of a system that pegs the yuan to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The United States, in an election year, says the yuan's pegged rate of 8.28 to the dollar gives China an unfair trade advantage, contributing to the record U.S. trade deficit.   \par \par \uc2 Not only is Japan more ambivalent in the absence of U.S.-style political considerations, Japanse businesses realise that any premature revaluation of the yuan could slow growth in China, an increasingly a important economic partner.   \par \par \uc2 "On balance, Japan, as much as the United States, could benefit from a yuan revaluation in the long term," said Yoshimasa Maruyama, economist at Mizuho Research Institute.   \par \par \uc2 "But because of the lack of political hype, people have a calmer view and realise it's crucial that China is stable."   \par \par \uc2 China has been invited to attend the meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers for the first time in recognition of its growing economic clout.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese business has mixed feelings about a yuan revaluation as China, apart from being a trade rival, is becoming a major manufacturing base for Japanese firms as well as a fast-growing market.   \par \par \uc2 Japan imports more from China than anywhere else, and China is Japan's second-largest export market after the United States.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have made it clear they don't want to upset China at the meeting, but have also suggested they have little influence over Washington's stand, particularly as the meeting is so close to a presidential election.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities also have reason to avoid the debate since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2003 and early 2004 to try to rein in the yen and protect an export-led recovery.   \par \par \uc2 DUBAI MISHAP   \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki restated Japan's position that China should decide what to do about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own terms.   \par \par \uc2 "As for the yuan, it is something that China should decide on its own, for its own benefit," he said, but he added that Japan was not without an opinion.   \par \par \uc2 "On that condition, though, we would like to tell China how we view the issue," he said without elaborating.   \par \par \uc2 On Monday, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said that Japan, "more than the United States", believed that China needed more time to revalue the yuan.   \par \par \uc2 China has a long-stated goal of allowing the yuan to move more freely but says this won't happen until it is fully confident about the health of its financial sector.   \par \par \uc2 But Eisuke Sakakibara, a professor at Keio University who previously attended G7 meetings as Japan's top financial diplomat, said he expected Japan to follow Washington's lead out of respect and avoid showing any discord.   \par \par \uc2 "It will all be about U.S. elections. Nothing that might be a disadvantage for the election will come out of it," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Kiichi Murashima, director of economic and market analysis at Nikko Citigroup, said Japan was likely to stay mum on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, knowing the issue could well backfire on Japan.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, along with their U.S. counterparts, called on China last year to loosen its grip on the yuan.   \par \par \uc2 But a statement issued after a G7 meeting a year ago in Dubai, which called for flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, triggered a surge in the value of the yen as investors interpreted it as a call for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not just China, to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "It has pursued the issue before and messed up. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has figured it's best not to say anything at all," Murashima said.   \par \par \uc2 The Dubai statement was altered at a following meeting to add a warning against excess volatility in exchange rates.   \par \par \uc2 This wording left room for the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is expected to be reiterated at the meeting.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent about 20 trillion yen ($180 billion) in 2003 and 15 trillion yen in the first three months of this year to try to rein in the yen.   \par \par \uc2 ($1=110.62 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040928e09s00c9p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
