\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar Burdened By Hole In The US Pocketbook  \b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2004\par 20:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Robert Flint \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The gap in the U.S. current account deficit is approaching an alarming 6% of gross domestic product, but investors and policymakers seem to be taking the record-wide deficit with nonchalance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 However, the shortfall in the external U.S. balance, amounting to $166.2 billion in the second quarter of 2004, places a huge burden on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though immediate market reaction on Tuesday remains muted.  \par \par \uc2 A few years ago, a deficit of 5% of GDP was seen as a danger line. Second-quarter figures released Tuesday by the Commerce Department indicate it stood at 5.71% of GDP, also an all-time record.  \par \par \uc2 The key issue is sustainability. The current account deficit isn't really a problem as long as it can be financed by capital flows from abroad. But should foreign investors become reluctant to put their money in dollar-denominated assets, the effects would be devastating for the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 "Our longer-term bearish (dollar) outlook reflects structural concerns about the ability of the U.S. to easily finance its growing current account deficit," wrote UBS, a top bank in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, in its morning email advisory.  \par \par \uc2 So far, there hasn't appeared to be any real difficulty in attracting the roughly $1.5 billion a day needed to cover the deficit. Much of that has to do with the willingness of Asian central banks, namely China's and Japan's, to buy U.S. bonds to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Deficit Sustained By Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Buying \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because the overwhelming component in the current account deficit is the shortfall in the trade in goods and services - $150.3 billion in the second quarter - nations with trade surpluses with the U.S. accumulate massive stacks of dollars. They are used to buy U.S. Treasury and agency bonds, thereby enabling the U.S. to sustain its current account deficit, at least in the short run.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By supporting the dollar through these purchases, Asian countries also maintain the competitiveness of their own export industries. The depreciation of the dollar that would be necessary to help bring U.S. external payments into balance is delayed in the process.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Through buying a huge amount of these bonds, China and Japan are keeping inflation and interest rates low in the U.S., said Sung Won Sohn, chief economist at Wells Fargo Bank in Minneapolis. "Clearly, this cannot go on forever."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to his bank's calculations, the U.S. will need to find $2 billion a day from abroad to cover the deficit in 2005. He's also concerned that future generations of Americans will face a 10-hour work day, with four of those hours going towards payments on the interest on external U.S. liabilities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Long-Term Problem That Must Be Resolved \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No one knows exactly at what point the burden of financing the deficit will inflict serious pain on the dollar and the U.S. economy. Federal Reserve officials, from Chairman Alan Greenspan on down, have acknowledged it is indeed a long-term problem that must be resolved, but don't see much cause for worry in the short term.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An obvious danger signal would be an unmistakable unwillingness by investors from abroad to place their money in the U.S. Already private sector investors have decided they hold too many U.S. assets, and should the Chinese and Japanese governments follow suit, the dollar will fall sharply as it adjusts to the decrease in demand, said Sohn.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moreover, the U.S. can't take the continued support of Asian central banks for granted, wrote Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at the Bank of New York, in an email update.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stopped \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  activities in mid-March and China could reduce its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if and when it decides to make its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and capital account more flexible, the current level of official demand for U.S. securities cannot be counted on to keep the (dollar) steady in the future," he stated.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's only hope for a long-term recovery is if the U.S. current account deficit is narrowed; net foreign direct investment turns positive; and investors from abroad step up purchases of U.S. equities, said Woolfolk.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net FDI and equity purchases are largely a reflection of the health of the economy. When activity heats up, both direct and equity investments is likely to follow. But bringing the current account deficit itself to more manageable proportions will prove a much stiffer task.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to Sohn, about 70% of the deficit stems from overconsumption, while 30% is attributable to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and trade factors. U.S. citizens are simply spending more than they can afford, leaving a big hole in the national pocketbook.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's not a new problem. Except for three quarters in the early 1990's when the U.S. ran current account surpluses because of payments from allies during the first Gulf War, it hasn't recorded a current account surplus in 22 years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nor will it go away easily or quickly. If there's anything more American than apple pie, it's got to be living beyond one's means.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Robert Flint, an assistant news editor, has covered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires for the past seven years and was previously Beijing bureau chief and chief correspondent in Scandinavia.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Robert Flint; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4408; robert.flint@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-14-04 1459ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040914e09e0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
