\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Leading Indicators Still Point To Growth  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2004\par 11:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese data released Tuesday pointed to continued economic expansion in the months ahead, calming worries that slowing global growth, high oil prices and falling wages may weigh on the nation's recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The index of leading indicators stood at 66.7 in July, staying above the boom-or-bust threshold of 50.0 for the eleventh straight month. A reading above 50 points to expansion over the coming six months while a level below 50 suggests contraction.  \par \par \uc2 "While the economy isn't without problems and can't produce (an index) reading of 100, the recovery is still on track, as shown by the numbers today," said Koichi Haji, chief economist at NLI Research Institute.  \par \par \uc2 The index of coincident indicators, which measures the current state of the economy, came to 77.8 in July as indicators for electricity usage, retail sales and smaller firms' revenues all improved. It was the third straight month the index was above 50.0.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data also showed that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit a record of $827.95 billion in August, though the increase was a modest $8.75 billion because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government has halted its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign since March.  \par \par \uc2 The optimistic data come as other recent data suggest the recovery in the world's second-largest economy remains on a firm footing. Data released Monday, for instance, showed that business investment jumped 10.7% on year in the April-June quarter, and that means Japan's gross domestic product data for the quarter will almost certainly be revised upward Friday.  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, not all analysts are convinced the economy can continue getting better at the same pace. They point to worrying signs in consumer spending, which makes up more than a half of Japan's economic activity.  \par \par \uc2 "Although the corporate sector has been doing all right so far, consumer spending will probably slow in coming months...as wages have yet to rebound," said Koji Fukaya, chief economic researcher at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 Those economists point to weaker-than-expected household spending, a surprising rise in the jobless rate and a fall in auto production for the domestic market in July.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese companies' average monthly wage fell for the third straight month in July as firms paid out smaller summer bonuses, government data showed last month.  \par \par \uc2 If consumers cut back on spending in the face of reduced paychecks, that could be bad news for Japan's economic recovery, which analysts say requires a recovery in domestic spending to offset weaker exports if the U.S. and Chinese economies slow later this year.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie [ 07-09-04 0954GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040907e097000j2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
