\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING:Volatile Oil Has Mkts Guessing  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,224 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2004\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 US SUMMARY: Tech Again Takes The Brunt  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA          10173.41  loss 8.33       dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ        1852.92   loss 7.80       dn 0.4% \par S&P 500       1105.09   gain 0.13          0.0% \par Dow Future    10167.00  loss 3.00          0.0% \par NASDAQ Future 1386.50   gain 3.50       up 0.3% \par S&P Future    1104.75   loss 0.25          0.0% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.21% down 0.05 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 A brokerage firm's negative outlook for the semiconductor industry pressured tech shares Thursday, while the broader market was little changed as a jump in jobless claims offset investors' relief over declining oil prices. Prices of Treasurys gained.  \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Late summer trading is known for being thin, but with the Republican National Convention set for New York next week, Wall Street is even less crowded than usual for late August. And as many traders prepared to take the week off and some money managers planned to reduce staff levels, the market's lack of conviction was reflected in low trading volumes. The drop in oil prices was a bright spot, however, even though analysts weren't sure it would hold.  \par \par \uc2 "A week ago, people were asking where the top in crude is and now they're asking where the bottom is," said Todd Clark, head of listed equity trading at Wells Fargo Securities. "It's a sea change that's welcome for equities, but against the backdrop of a big event next week that will keep players away from the market, it's tough to get too excited."  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: U.S. Treasurys settled higher within the confines of their recent narrow trading ranges Thursday amid quiet dealings.  \par \par \uc2 Traders characterized activity as being light with many participants already on vacation or planning to take time off between now and Labor Day weekend. Ahead of the Republican National Convention starting Aug. 30, there was talk of Treasury buying by index managers as liquidity for month end next week will likely contract.  \par \par \uc2 "It's going to be thin for the next week," said Lundy Wright, head of Treasury trading at Nomura Securities in New York, adding that many are waiting until next Friday when the August employment report is due out before they leap back into the market.  \par \par \uc2 Ralph Axel, fixed income strategist at HSBC in New York said, "The market is expecting nothing will happen until the payroll report." He noted that the 10-year note yield was back to the 4.22% level where it closed after the lackluster July employment report was released Aug. 6.  \par \par \uc2 "The market has been trading in a tight range since the last payroll report and moving up and down on changes in the oil price," he said.  \par \par \uc2 October crude oil futures gyrated around $43 a barrel Thursday, mounting a recovery from their earlier low of $42.50/bbl and ultimately settling at $43.10/bbl.  \par \par \uc2 ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Edge Up, As Does Crude  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par USD-Yen             109.49       loss  0.10    dn 0.09% \par AUD-USD             0.7057       gain  0.0020  up 0.3% \par Nikkei 225          11173.07     gain  43.74   up 0.4% \par Hang Seng           12794.90     gain  10.51   up 0.1% \par JGB Yield           1.5750%          down 0.0250 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo stocks rose Friday morning amid bargain-hunting of blue-chip issues related to the domestic economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Select technology and banking shares opened higher to nudge the benchmark Nikkei up slightly higher in early trading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bargain-hunting of blue chip issues outside of the technology sector helped push the index up in later trading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the morning's advancers were Takeda Chemical Industries, tire maker Bridgestone and Mistubishi Estate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors didn't react immediately to an increase in Japan's unemployment rate for the first time since January.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government announced before the market's open Friday that the jobless rate was up 0.3 point at 4.9% in July - higher than economists' forecasts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October Nymex crude rose 17 cents at $43.27/bbl on Access after slipping in N.Y., amid speculative buying on doubts that a peace accord in the embattled Iraqi town of Najaf would last.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks May Slump At Open  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-USD    1.2112      gain  0.0008  up 0.05% \par Stlg-USD    1.7980      gain  0.0020  up 0.1% \par USD-Franc   1.2715      loss  0.0007  dn 0.06% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of European shares are expected to kick off more or less unchanged, while prices of European government bonds may tick higher, with the euro up a notch.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: The FTSE 100 is seen opening flat at 4453, while the CAC 40 is expected to start four points higher at 3633, according to spreadbettor Cantor Index. It calls the DAX also unchanged, at 3832.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were boosted by a rash of upbeat corporate newsflow Thursday and lower oil prices also helped whet investors' appetite for equities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is no denying that the fall in oil has quickly turned sentiment that was previously irrationally gloomy," said analysts at Barclays Private Clients. However they cautioned that the market recovery is quite narrowly based and is on thin volumes.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley remain bullish on equity market valuations on a 12-month view but they too are advising clients to avoid becoming bullish too soon. "We are aware of the risks on a 1-2 month view," they said, noting the potential for oil prices to surprise and for U.S. economic data to disappoint.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Prices of Europen government bonds may tend higher at the opening, ahead of a watershed week coming up.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The focus is firmly on next week's payrolls, to the extent that oil and equities have become a bit of a sideshow," said one broker with a European bank in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts held steady, despite a sharp fall in oil prices, with the session's German Ifo and U.S. jobless claims data providing little obvious impetus.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The German Ifo business climate index eased to 95.3 in August from July's 95.6. Analysts had expected a 95.1 reading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The decline points to further weakness in German domestic demand and a slower rate of euro-zone industrial output growth, said strategists at Barclays Capital.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's also likely to cement expectations that European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  interest rates will remain steady at 2% for the rest of this year.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts what could be a light session on a slightly higher level from the New York close.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Slightly higher-than-expected jobless claims put some pressure on the dollar Thursday, but the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains in the middle of recent ranges against major rivals.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After gaining strength early in the week, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell back slightly on Wednesday and Thursday. Still, trading is light and market participants noted that all of the week's activity was only a prelude to Friday, when they hope that comments from Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan and the release of U.S. economic data will lend \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets some conviction.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another factor in Thursday's trading, according to some traders, was resumed selling of the euro versus the yen. Though the recent selloff in the euro met with a minor correction Wednesday, the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still down more than 3% versus the yen over the past eight trading sessions, and it broke through major technical support to get there.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It has also been a factor behind a lesser decline of a bit more than 2.2% in the euro versus the dollar over the same period, as the the dollar has been steady versus the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We saw a resumption of the euro/yen selling trade, which in large part dictated what we saw in dollar versus yen and dollar versus euro," said David Leaver, senior trader at Gain Capital in Warren, New Jersey.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While traders balked at sending the euro below Y132.30 level as buyers stepped in, traders still have their eyes on Y132.00 as a make-or-break level for the euro. If prices can poke below that, they look for further selling toward the 130.00 level, said Leaver.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he noted that Y132.00 may prove hard to break, with talk that at least one Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to be a willing buyer of euros at Y132.00. For now he said, there is no expectation that the yen's rally versus the euro, which has come hard and fast, will prompt the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets, as it did earlier this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As for the euro, with the dollar sitting right in the middle of its recent range, the risks to the upside and the downside are evenly balanced, wrote UBS analysts in a research report.  \f2