\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar and yen seen benefiting from equity inflows.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2004\par 16:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 24 (Reuters) - A renewed appetite for equities could impact global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in coming weeks, with the dollar and yen well-placed to benefit, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 "Absent negative shocks, the rise in the Dow and positive upswing in equities could certainly put a floor under the dollar," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York. "Equity outflow from the U.S. seems to have subsided in the last two weeks."  \par \par \uc2 Optimism over the global economic recovery and the dollar, which was knocked by weak U.S. employment and trade data earlier this month, may be returning.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices have eased back from their record highs near $50 a barrel in the past few days, while bond yields have ticked up again and gold, traditionally a safe-haven asset in times of economic or geopolitical uncertainty, is off its highs too.  \par \par \uc2 This provides a more positive environment for the dollar, though terrorism fears, concerns about the strength of the U.S. economy and the massive U.S. external deficits requiring funding, all remain bearish factors for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A sustained stock market recovery, however, would help alleviate that pressure.  \par \par \uc2 Since plumbing year-to-date lows on Aug. 12, the day it was announced the U.S. economy created only 32,000 new jobs in July, the Dow Jones industrials have recovered 3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's trade weighted index has climbed back to 89.50 from a month low at 87.70. Meanwhile, the euro has given back all the gains it got from the payrolls number and was trading at $1.2130 early Tuesday, compared with $1.2375 only last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The flow of investment into U.S. stocks, considered riskier assets than bonds and gold, and a barometer of confidence in the economy, is by no means a tide though. Proprietary data from UBS show that a small net $36 million flowed out of U.S. equity markets last week.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the four-week moving average was a net inflow to equities of $81 million from $5 million the previous week. UBS sees around 9 percent of global secondary market equity flows, making it the second-largest dealer in the world.  \par \par \uc2 Custody holdings data from the Bank of New York show that non-U.S. investors bought a net $275 million of U.S. equities last week. The buying continued into early this week, Bank of New York's Woolfolk said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts at Royal Bank of Scotland, however, question the link between the dollar and U.S. equities. They argue that the only time the link has been in evidence was 1998-2000, the tail end of the stock market boom. The huge U.S. current account deficit and relatively high U.S. valuations compared with other regions should limit investor demand for U.S. stocks, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "Going forward, dollar strength is unlikely to be based on flows into equities, and equity market strength may actually prove more generally negative for the dollar," Bank of Scotland wrote in a recent research note.  \par \par \uc2 That's in large part because of something they and other analysts more widely agree on: Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Moves in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the yen, tend to be more closely led by stock market moves. The strength of Asian equities this year shows that global fund managers increasingly view those markets as a rival to Wall Street.  \par \par \uc2 "We could see a resumption of the flows back into Japanese equities that made the yen so strong at the start of the year," said Marvin Barth, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London.  \par \par \uc2 Despite unprecedented yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year, the yen recovered to near 107 against the dollar by late June, before losing some ground again recently as oil prices rose.  \par \par \uc2 "That's what we've seen, this return of buying into Japanese assets and I think that's why dollar/yen hasn't kept pace with the oil price," said Patrick Brodie, chief dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The yen had until recently shown an inverse link to the rising oil price because Japan's economy is entirely dependent on oil imports. But as oil eases further away from the $50 mark and the Nikkei starts to pick up, it remains to be seen if the yen follows.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040824e08o00cyu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
