\par\par \b \uc2 IMF Ups Japan's '04 Econ Growth Forecast To 4.5% Vs 3.4%  \b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2004\par 16:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The International Monetary Fund on Wednesday painted a rosy economic picture of Japan, significantly raising its growth forecast for this year to 4.5% from 3.4% in April.  \par \par \uc2 The new forecast was a further upgrading from the one made by the IMF mission team sent to Tokyo in late May, when its chief said the world's second largest economy would likely grow at around 4.0% this year.  \par \par \uc2 Japan will keep its growth momentum next year, albeit subsiding a little, with growth likely to reach 2.5% in the year, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 "There are clear indications that Japan's longstanding economic problems have eased. Growth is rebounding, deflation is diminishing...near term growth prospects are favorable," the international lender said in its annual "Article IV" report on Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Although the current recovery was first driven by corporate investment and exports, the IMF said, it sees private consumption now contributing more to growth - an ingredient necessary to put the country back on self-sustained growth.  \par \par \uc2 But the IMF cautioned about a downside risk to this scenario from possible external shocks, such as a potential sharp rise in global interest rates, and urged Tokyo to continue its restructuring efforts and appropriate policies.  \par \par \uc2 In this sense, the IMF board welcomed the Bank of Japan's commitment to the current ultra-easy monetary policy, until the country's deflation ends.  \par \par \uc2 "Directors saw the current accommodative policy stance as appropriate and...further increases in the current account operating target could be used if needed to reinforce the policy commitment," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 Though its rate of decline is slowing, Tokyo's core consumer price index has kept falling for the last five years. And in order to arrest this fall, the BOJ has adopted the so-called "quantitative easing" policy, providing money enough to the market for private banks' current accounts at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain at high levels.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF expects to see CPI inflation "ebbing to zero" by the end of 2005.  \par \par \uc2 But the IMF directors were divided over the BOJ adopting inflation targeting as a tool to effectively communicate the bank's policy at times of diminishing deflation.  \par \par \uc2 "While some directors supported quantification of a mid-term inflation objective, a number of others considered such an objective premature at this point," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF also urged continued efforts to clean up the country's banking system.  \par \par \uc2 While acknowledging that the country's major banks are "on track" to achieve the goal of reducing their non-performing loans to about 4% of their total lending by next March, the IMF directors urged the authorities to take similar policy steps toward regional banks, so that they, too, would accelerate their non-performing loan disposals.  \par \par \uc2 Regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the tool that Tokyo employs to prevent the yen from rising, IMF directors warned against massive use of the strategy.  \par \par \uc2 "With exchange rate risks now more balanced, such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were not seen as desirable," the IMF statement said. Many directors think additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be warranted only "if significant upward pressures (on the yen) reemerged" and urged that "the exchange rate be fully market determined."  \par \par \uc2 During the 15 months through March, Japan spent almost Y35 trillion, or about $318 billion, to buy the dollar in attempts to keep the yen at levels favorable to Japan's exports.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9285; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com [ 08-11-04 1000ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040811e08b00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
