\par\par \b \uc2 = \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: As Bear Clouds Gather Dollar Has Few Friends  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Vames  \par \uc2 1,044 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2004\par 03:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar has very few friends these days and it doesn't look like anybody is going to stretch out the hand of support anytime soon.  \par \par \uc2 Data released from the Commodities Trading Commission late Friday confirmed the sentiment that's been building for weeks: virtually everybody is bearish on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Indeed net dollar short positions for non-commercial, or speculative accounts rose sharply in the week ending July 13 versus the euro, sterling, and Swiss franc in particular, leaping to $11 billion, making for the most extreme positioning versus those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it hit $11.5 billion in October 1999.  \par \par \uc2 Typically, such a large wave of bearishness aimed at a single asset class could be an opportune time to start embracing the counter-trend. But given current expectations for the Federal Reserve to lift interest rates from their current lows only modestly - and the structural forces working against the dollar - most strategists are hard-pressed to say anything kind about it.  \par \par \uc2 'There is always the idea that when everybody is bearish, the danger becomes the risk of retreat,' said Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. However, the current force undermining the dollar is more than just a speculative mob pouncing on a vulnerable victim, said Lynch.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been under pressure against many rivals for some time, hitting multi-month lows against the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday in the wake of lower than expected core inflation data. It did recover some of that ground on Monday - the euro was trading around $1.2436 late in the day, below $1.2448 late Friday in New York while sterling was trading at $1.8682 versus $1.8722 - but traders said the gains lacked conviction and the U.S. unit remained under pressure against many peripheral rivals.  \par \par \uc2 The weakness has come in lockstep with a lowering of U.S. market interest rates that's come as the uncertainty about Federal Reserve policy expectations has cleared.  \par \par \uc2 'The link between interest rate expectations and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very strongly in play right now, especially since we are at this turning point for the interest rate cycle,' said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar's Loss Is Treasurys' Gain  \par \par \uc2 Data seen for June have to a certain extent reshaped economists' optimism for growth by showing wavering levels of job creation, industrial production, retail sales, and further signs of low inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Against this backdrop, Treasurys have taken heart from the prospect of the Fed lifting the current 1.25% federal funds rate only slowly. Some investors are even moving back into riskier trades in emerging market and speculative grade bonds. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors are choosing to focus on what they perceive as the negative fallout in this scenario and argue that modest rate hikes won't increase the attractiveness of holding the dollar relative to higher-yielding alternatives such as the Australian dollar or sterling.  \par \par \uc2 Testimony on monetary policy from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan on Tuesday and Wednesday isn't expected to shed significant new light on the Fed's interest rate intentions or on the economy itself, and markets are doomed to wait until the Aug. 6 jobs report to get their next major economic signpost.  \par \par \uc2 Until then, the dollar looks either neutral or a sell, says Lynch. 'You have to think that if people were going to start buying dollars near these levels, they'd have already started doing it,' he said.  \par \par \uc2 Of course, that doesn't exclude the potential for some near-term corrections, said Lynch. Because the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tied up with so many bearish trades and has lost ground in recent weeks, there's always the potential for some profit taking.  \par \par \uc2 That could come on the back of technical factors, such as a failure by the euro to cross through resistance at $1.2480 from its current $1.2436. It could also come if some July data, such as regional and national Institute of Supply Management reports, consumer confidence indicators or others give enough sign of strength to embolden \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors.  \par \par \uc2 Yen's A Different Story  \par \par \uc2 The yen and the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that track it are a different story because of the trade surplus versus deficit dynamic, said Durrant. The dollar's weakness versus the yen, he added, is more of a textbook play involving relative economies, and the yen would likely be even stronger versus the dollar if not for the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, even if latent dollar bulls get what they want - a sign that the Fed will raise rates swiftly - they might be in for a surprise.  \par \par \uc2 Theories of comparative interest rates say the expectation for aggressive rate hikes is bullish for the dollar because investors will flock toward the higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the reality of funding the current account deficit means the U.S. needs a constant flow of investment from overseas, and the losses that rate-hikes will trigger in U.S. stock and bond markets may at least temporarily curb the enthusiasm of overseas investors.  \par \par \uc2 'Higher rates do end up being positive for the dollar - and we will probably get a bit of a rebound if and when the Fed is determined to be on the aggressive track,' said Jason Bonanca, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston.  \par \par \uc2 But once rates are actively rising, it won't be a full-time party for the dollar, he warned. Indeed, rising rates will mean Treasurys will be incurring price declines and the many rate-sensitive sectors of the stock market are likely to languish for that period, turning off overseas investors.  \par \par \uc2 'The reaction to expectations is one thing, but once the tightening is actually happening, there are transitions in the markets that people will want to avoid,' Bonanca said.  \par \par \uc2 (Steven Vames covers \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has also covered equities in Los Angeles and the Treasurys market and fixed income industry in New York.)  \par \par \uc2 -Steven Vames; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-5063; steven.vames@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020040720e07k001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
