\par\par \b \uc2 Ex-MOF Sakakibara: Expect Dollar At Y120 By Year-End  \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2004\par 06:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's economy, stock markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have already peaked and will likely decline by the end of this year, an influential former Japanese Finance Ministry official said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 As a result, the dollar, which is currently trading around Y109.50, will likely rise to around Y120 by the end of the year, Eisuke Sakakibara, a former vice finance minister for international affairs, said at the Foreign Correspondents Club of Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar at Y120 is not unnatural... and not an unacceptable level for the U.S.," he said. [ 15-07-04 0458GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara said that global financial markets are starting to experience a "paradigm shift," as an end to deflationary, or disinflationary, pressures leads to a global tightening of monetary policy to tackle inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 "The question being asked a year or two ago, up to very recently ... was what country was less vulnerable to deflation or disinflation," Sakakibara said.  \par \par \uc2 "The question being asked now, however, after the paradigm shift ... is, what country is less vulnerable to the regime of inflation and higher interest rates." he said.  \par \par \uc2 Amid a backdrop of global credit tightening, economies that are most vulnerable are the ones with the most debt, such as Japan's with its huge public-sector debt, and the U.S. with mushrooming consumer debt.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's situation is also unlikely to be helped by the fact that the Japanese public has become disillusioned by Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's failure to deliver on his reform pledges. A decline in his popularity will likely result in him becoming a "lame duck" and lead to a period of political stagnation.  \par \par \uc2 The Eurozone, however, is less vulnerable to higher interest rates than either the U.S. or Japan, as it has less debt and it's economy is "better balanced," Sakakibara said.  \par \par \uc2 In this sense, the euro will likely strengthen in the medium-term against the dollar and the yen, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Over the next year, the descending order of the world's three major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely be the euro, the dollar and the yen, said Sakakibara, who served as vice finance minister from 1997 to 1999.  \par \par \uc2 But in the event the dollar bucks fundamentals and collapses for whatever reason, including fears of terrorist attacks, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling below Y100.00, or possibly even Y105.00, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, the MOF official who is currently in Sakakibara's old post, "is more aggressive" than his predecessor, Zembei Mizoguchi, when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Sakakibara, who earned the monicker "Mr. Yen" for his ability to drive the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up or down with barbed comments and sometimes aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 While Sakakibara didn't forget to add that Watanabe was probably not as aggressive as he himself, if the dollar falls sharply, "Watanabe will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite aggressively," to stop the dollar's fall, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par 