\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves build-up stalls in second qtr.  \b0\par\par\uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2004\par 05:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SINGAPORE, July 9 (Reuters) - Asia's stunning accumulation\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves stalled in the second quarter,\par with holdings rising just 0.2 percent to $2.160 trillion, the\par latest figures from central banks show.\par     Excluding China, Asian reserves rose just $4.0 billion in\par the second quarter. That is a massive slowdown from the first\par quarter when they rose by $210.7 billion.\par     The figures do not include China because it has not\par released its reserve holdings for April, May or June. The\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to release June quarter holdings later\par this month.\par     In the first three months of the year, China's reserves\par rose over $36 billion to $439.8 billion. China is the world's\par second-largest holder of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves after Japan.\par  \par     In the second quarter, reserves fell in Japan, Hong Kong,\par Singapore, Indonesia, the Philippines and Pakistan.\par     Outside Japan, where reserves have risen by 21.5 percent\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 since the end of 2003, Malaysia and India have recorded the  \par \par \uc2 biggest percent increases this year. But in June, Indian  \par \par \uc2 reserves fell slightly from May and Malaysian reserves were  \par \par \uc2 flat.  \par \par \uc2 The June quarter pause comes after a sustained rise that  \par \par \uc2 prompted international criticism and worries that Asia's  \par \par \uc2 reserves could potentially destabilise financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 In the 18 months to March 2004, Asian reserve holdings  \par \par \uc2 including China rose by $780 billion to $2.156 trillion - a  \par \par \uc2 rise of almost 57 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The rapid accumulation largely reflected the region's  \par \par \uc2 determination to stem gains in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a weakening  \par \par \uc2 U.S. dollar and protect export competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 Many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaked against the dollar in late  \par \par \uc2 March or early April, and then weakened as financial markets  \par \par \uc2 positioned for the start of a tightening cycle in the United  \par \par \uc2 States. The U.S. Federal Reserve increased the Fed funds rate  \par \par \uc2 last week.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during  \par \par \uc2 the second quarter after spending a record 14.8 trillion yen  \par \par \uc2 ($135 billion) in the first quarter of 2004 to cap the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The end of a sharp rally in Asian stock markets has eased,  \par \par \uc2 and in some cases reversed, the investment inflows which were  \par \par \uc2 adding to the pressure for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise. This  \par \par \uc2 has also lessened the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve holdings:\par                Reserves       As at      end-2003   percent\par                   $ bln                     $ bln    change\par  Japan            818.0      Jun 30         673.5     +21.5\par  China*           439.8      Mar 31         403.3      +9.1\par  Taiwan           230.1      Jun 30         206.6     +11.4\par  South Korea      167.0      Jun 30         155.4      +7.5\par  Hong Kong        120.8      Jun 30         118.4      +2.0\par  India            119.4      Jun 25         100.6     +18.7\par  Singapore        101.6      Jun 30          96.3      +5.5\par  Malaysia          53.9      Jun 30          44.9     +20.0\par  Thailand          43.2      Jun 25          42.1      +2.6\par  Indonesia         34.9      Jun 30          36.3      -3.9\par  Philippines       16.2      Jun 30          16.8      -3.6\par  Pakistan          12.4      Jun 26          12.0      +3.3\par  Bangladesh         2.7      Jun 30           2.6      +3.8\par  Total          2,160.0                   1,908.8     +13.2\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *In late December, China used $45 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 reserves - about 10 percent of its holdings at that time - to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 bail out two banks. Those funds are not included in this  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 figure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: central banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040709e079000eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
