\par\par \b \uc2 Cantos Q&A: ABN Amro Senior  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Strategist  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2004\par 10:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following is an unedited interview with the ABN Amro Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Shahab Jalinoos, produced by Cantos. Cantos is a U.K.-based financial and corporate information provider.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar to end year stronger  \par \par \uc2 US rate rises to benefit dollar  \par \par \uc2 Q.  \par \par \uc2 How much further do you expect the Fed to tighten U.S. rates and where will this take the dollar?  \par \par \uc2 A.  \par \par \uc2 We expect at ABN Amro the Fed to hike rates up to 2% by the end of 2004, up to 4 per cent by the end of 2005. So that's significant further tightening to come. This is as part of a normalisation of U.S. interest rates from very low levels that were needed to deal with the aftermath of the tech stock bubble bursting. Over-investment and over-capacity needed to be washed out of the economy. And in effect, because we've had a few inflationary numbers coming through right now that have been basically surprises for the Fed - they've been in the higher end of what they would have expected - that's part of the reason we see this accelerated move towards normalising rates.  \par \par \uc2 Now to some extent that's already priced into the U.S. interest rate curve. So it may not necessarily be a huge shock to the market when you see these kind of rate hikes come through.  \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, as far as the dollar is concerned, part of the reason the investment community has been happy to sell dollars is that U.S. interest rates have been far below rates in other major areas like Europe, or the U.K., or Australia and that's turned the dollar into a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As these rates normalise in the U.S., you're going to see a contraction in rate differentials, let's say between the U.S. and Europe. In fact, the rate differential move in the U.S.'s favour. Once that happens, from a funding perspective, it's going to be very difficult to use the dollar as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 For example, a lot of corporates have hedged a lot of their dollar exposure partly because of the fact that it has been cheap to hedge because U.S. interest rates have been so low. They will have to buy back some of those hedges and that will create a dollar-positive flow. So we expect the dollar to benefit from those kind of moves.  \par \par \uc2 Q.  \par \par \uc2 Will these rate hikes in the U.S., though, offset fears over the current account deficit?  \par \par \uc2 A.  \par \par \uc2 When you look at a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the dollar, there is two sides to it at the moment. There is a cyclical story which is tied into this interest rate development that I've talked about, which is dollar-positive. We've normally seen in previous cycles when the Fed has hiked rates that the dollar has ended the year stronger from where it started the year. So there is one exception to that which was 1994 when the Fed shocked the market in the way it hiked rates. It created a bit of a panic. It hurt the dollar. But generally, when the Fed hikes rates, the dollar does well.  \par \par \uc2 So the cyclical factor will work in the dollar's favour. Now what you allude to is a structural problem that the dollar has which is primarily highlighted by the current account deficit, but also includes the fiscal deficit and together they're called the twin deficits problem.  \par \par \uc2 At the moment, it's too early to say that the structural problem is going to go away and that may slow the speed at which the dollar can rally. At the same time, though, we've done some research that shows that there are early signs now that maybe the worst is over as far as the structural problems go in the sense that the pace of deterioration of the current account deficit has slowed down substantially. It is the same with the fiscal deficit. If you see a bottoming out of the deficit, then maybe the worst case expectations of some structural dollar bears in the market they turn not to materialise. And again, they may have to reduce some of their dollar short positions on the back of that.  \par \par \uc2 As far as the current account deficit, for example, we've seen that U.S. exports growth has been very good in the past few months and U.S. imports growth has lagged somewhat the extent to which we would have thought they would come through given how strong U.S. growth has been. So it may be that the dollar has already fallen enough, basically, to make the U.S. economy competitive and if that is the case, then there is going to be a natural improvement in the trade balance and maybe the fears about the structural side are overblown.  \par \par \uc2 So the combination of an improved cyclical story and a potential improvement in the structural story as well bodes well for the dollar. But it could take a few months for the market as a whole to take that view as well. [ 08-07-04 0828GMT ]  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact of slow European growth  \par \par \uc2 Q.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB is lagging the current rate tightening cycle. Does that mean that the euro will weaken?  \par \par \uc2 A.  \par \par \uc2 We think that does mean the euro will weaken because you will see rate differentials move against Europe. For example, at the moment we have a base rate in the euro area of 2% and U.S. rates are at 1.25%. We expect European rates to remain at 2% until the end of 2004. We expect U.S. base rates to move to 4%. So that will flip the premium that Europe has on the interest rate side of around 75 basis points to a 200 basis point deficit. That's just looking at base rates.  \par \par \uc2 Now what that means is that players who currently look at the dollar as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may actually start looking at the euro as one when that reversal happens in interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 There is a broader story as well and that is that interest rates themselves are a function of the relative growth rate of the U.S. and Europe and obviously the fact that U.S. rates are expected to rise so dramatically and that we expect European rates to stay flat, suggests that we expect very, very substantial growth out performance by the U.S. relative to Europe as well. And that is going to act as a natural magnet for foreign capital to move into the U.S. economy. And again, that would undermine the euro to some extent as well.  \par \par \uc2 So the upshot is that yes, we think if Europe lags the rate hike cycle, that will pull the euro lower.  \par \par \uc2 Q.  \par \par \uc2 How much by?  \par \par \uc2 A.  \par \par \uc2 It is difficult to put an absolute sum in terms of percentages as to the impact a widening rate differential should have because there is other factors that affect the market as well. Like geopolitical risk, or trade movements, trade flows or M&A activity. All these factors come together so it is hard to put just one number and say, well for every 100 basis points in rate differential widening against Europe you're going to get a 3% move in the euro. We can't really say with that kind of accuracy. But what we can say though is that we have models that suggest that on the basis of underlying flows alone, a fairer value for euro dollar would be around 1.10 as opposed to the 1.20, 1.25 levels that we're at right now.  \par \par \uc2 Now to some extent, the reason we're so high is based on this interest rate argument. There are other stories in there as well though, like maybe the market over-pricing the structural risks facing the U.S. economy. But what we can say is if both of these factors are washed out, let's say in the next 6-12 months, that would suggest the euro-dollar should fall towards the 1.10 level and that's on a six month forecast for that rate.  \par \par \uc2 A lasting Japanese rebound?  \par \par \uc2 Q.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's economic rebound seems to be boosting the yen. Do you see this being sustained though?  \par \par \uc2 A.  \par \par \uc2 We think there is a measured difference between this recovery that we're seeing in Japan and the very short-lived recoveries that we saw in the 1990s. The prior recoveries were very much based on exports and government spending and there was a consistent theme of the corporate sector being on very shaky ground with its debt problem and a consumer not being willing to spend because of insecurity over jobs and other issues like that.  \par \par \uc2 So this time round the difference is that exports are not really playing the same pivotal role in the recovery that they did before. They did at the onset of the recovery, but at this stage they're not really the critical issue any more. Government spending has really not been a factor at all. There has been a relative fiscal discipline in Japan over the past couple of years. So what we're seeing now is a rebounding corporate profitability in Japan that's driving investment and that itself has improved job prospects as well in Japan and improved consumer sentiment as well.  \par \par \uc2 So for the first time in a long, long time, the drivers of growth look to be investment growth and potentially consumer spending growth and that's a much healthier underlying situation for the Japanese economy than existed before. What that suggests is that the dependence of Japan on global growth will to some extent be lower than it has been in the past. It still will be dependent. For example, exports to China have been rising at very high rates recently and a Chinese slowdown would potentially be a problem for Japan. But it's not the be all and end all like it might have been let's say a couple of years ago.  \par \par \uc2 So because the Japanese economy is standing on its own feet more than it has in the past, the vulnerability of the yen has somewhat diminished because if there is a shock to the global economy, what that means is that Japan, because it is not quite so export dependent, will not be forced to devalue the yen in the same way it would have done in the past. And if growth in the global economy proceeds well, what that means is that capital can continue to flow into Japan because Japan itself at the moment is, in terms of growth rates, is at the forefront of that recovery. And if capital flows into Japan, and Japan also runs the trade surplus as it is doing right now, then underlying flows are going to be very yen positive and the only barrier towards yen strength will be \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 And again, if you start to see signs that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deflation problem is passing, and there are early signs of that, then it's going to be difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make the case to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  very strongly in the dollar/yen rate and they may back off a little bit.  \par \par \uc2 So for that reason, we think that the current growth that we see in Japan is sustainable and is going to benefit the yen as well going forward. [ 08-07-04 0830GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Q.  \par \par \uc2 So how far then do you think the yen could climb against the dollar?  \par \par \uc2 A.  \par \par \uc2 The problem as far as the Japanese authorities go is that they don't want to see the yen appreciate too quickly because corporate Japan has been sending out signals that while it's comfortable with the idea of yen appreciation, it would struggle if the yen were to appreciate too quickly.  \par \par \uc2 So, as long as we get an orderly appreciation then everybody will be fine. For that reason we think that we're going to see a relatively gradual move higher in the yen towards 1.05 by the end of 2004 and then towards 1.02 by the middle of next year.  \par \par \uc2 So the answer to your question, in brief, is that although the underlying flows are still very positive for Japan, there is going to be a natural caution in the market to try and drive the yen up too fast because of this knowledge that corporate Japan would disapprove of too fast an appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese devaluation scenario  \par \par \uc2 Q.  \par \par \uc2 What's the picture going forward on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 A.  \par \par \uc2 This is a topic that has been a major issue so far in 2004. We started the year with a market betting through the forward market in China that the renminbi would appreciate against the dollar, that there would be a revaluation at some point during this year.  \par \par \uc2 Now in recent weeks that story has changed somewhat and now the market really isn't looking for a move this year at all. There is a number of reasons for this. Partly it is because the pressure that the dollar was under at the start of the year generally has abated somewhat.  \par \par \uc2 At the start of the year there was a view that because of the U.S. current account deficit, the dollar would have to keep falling, but that it was falling against the wrong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was falling against the euro for example. The U.S. doesn't really have that big a trade deficit against the European area. But it wasn't falling against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like China where the actual problem lies in terms of why the US has a big trade deficit. So there was a view that the Chinese would be forced for political reasons to allow a revaluation as a means of dealing with some of these structural problems that exist.  \par \par \uc2 Now because the dollar has managed to find its feet again, that issue has taken a bit of a back seat.  \par \par \uc2 On the other side as well, on the Chinese side itself, recent measures that China has taken to try and slow its growth rate because of potential inflation problems, potential bad loans problems etc., have convinced the market that we may not see quite the same growth rates in China that we have been seeing recently. You may not seem the same degree of capital inflows into China that we have done in recent months and that will in itself alleviate some of the upper pressure on the Chinese renminbi.  \par \par \uc2 So it's the combination of the dollar finding its feet generally and China taking steps to try and slow down its growth rate that has led the market to believe that maybe we won't see a Chinese renminbi revaluation this year.  \par \par \uc2 Still though, our best guess is that next year, by the middle of next year or in the second half of next year sorry, we will see a Chinese renminbi revaluation of between 3 to 5%. Partly that's because we still expect Chinese growth to outperform US growth fairly substantially. We still expect capital flows to be highly favourable for China. We don't expect the US trade deficit with China to contract materially. So there is always going to be political pressure on China from the U.S. side as well to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 And there is still an outside chance that if the Democrats win the election in the U.S., the new Kerry administration will take a harder line on what they see as \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation by China. So far, Kerry has taken a relatively tough line on China. There has been a degree of China bashing from the Democratic party generally about Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and the impact it has on US jobs. If the Kerry team, upon winning the election, decides to make that a key political issue, then that may force the Chinese to have to think about revaluation sooner rather than later.  \par \par \uc2 So that risk is there as well and for all those reasons, we think that, although we're not going to see revaluation this year, it is a fair bet to say we'll see one in the second half of next year.  \par \par \uc2 A video/audio version is available free by logging on to  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cantos.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.cantos.com}}}\uc2 , along with other current financial and business interviews. Contact: Tony McMahon +44(0)20 7936 1333. E-mail: info@cantos.com.  \par \par \uc2 Dow Jones & Co. has no responsibility for the information in this article, or the Cantos Web site. [ 08-07-04 0831GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040708e078000cf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
