\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: S. Korea Loads Up On Ammo To Fight Won's Climb  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2004\par 04:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Hye-Seung Seo \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--South Korea is stocking up on ammunition to shoot down a rising won, taking no chances even as market participants trim their bets on further gains by the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The government wants to issue up to 11 trillion won ($1=KRW1,153) in treasurys to sell for dollars in the second half, on top of the KRW7.8 trillion already allocated for won-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy say.  \par \par \uc2 "Raising the limit by such an extent means having such that much greater power," Choi Joong-Kyung, director-general of the ministry's International Finance bureau, told Dow Jones Newswires Friday. "And we'll use it to the maximum limit if necessary."  \par \par \uc2 Seoul is signaling it wants to be as relentless as Japan in supporting the dollar, just as foreign banks are becoming a tad less won-bullish and the options market positions for a halt to the won's rise.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is testing April's four-year lows, but few doubt that Seoul is serious about defending the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it wants to keep above KRW1,150. Around 0200 GMT Monday the dollar was at KRW1,153.4.  \par \par \uc2 The won has been showing signs of strength lately not because of any improvement in the stubbornly sluggish Korean economy but because of perkiness in the yen, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the won typically tracks. That's especially bad news for Korea, where exports - which largely compete with those from Japan - have been the sole source of strength in an economy still struggling to recover from a recession in the first half of 2003. A stronger won makes Korean cars, electronics equipment and other goods less competitive on world markets.  \par \par \uc2 "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remains resolute," Choi said. "We'll use every won to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from speculators and from moves that go against economic fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 The government last year twice raised the limit on treasurys earmarked for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more than tripling the year's ceiling to KRW12.8 trillion from KRW3.8 trillion. And it spent every won it was allocated to support the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 By the end of this month, the government will already have sold KRW7 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, leaving just KRW800 million of its current allocation. If Parliament approves the new request, the authorities will have KRW2 trillion a month to sell for dollars for the last five months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a mind game," said a dealer at a local bank. "It's to scare players who may be thinking that the government has run out of ammunition."  \par \par \uc2 Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy brings to mind \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recently ended yen-selling campaign. Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  war chest for this year will total 2.8% of the country's gross domestic product - on a par with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cumulative Y140 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -budget ceiling, which comes to about 2.5% of Japanese GDP. Japan dumped a record Y35 trillion on the market in the 15 months through March - making any speculator think twice about buying yen - before abruptly halting the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign as the Japanese economy improved strongly.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Second Thoughts On Won Rise \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intentionally or not, Seoul's fresh campaign against a won rise comes as the market is having second thoughts whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can keep strengthening.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Big foreign banks still expect the dollar to fall below the KRW1,140-KRW1,150 band that Seoul has fiercely defended for the last four years, but they have recently become a tad less dollar-bearish. Citibank raised its six-month dollar target to KRW1,135 from KRW1,120, J.P. Morgan upped its forecast to KRW1,120 from KRW1,108, and Bank of America now predicts the dollar's fall will stop at KRW1,120 instead of the previously expected KRW1,100.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the dollar approaches the critical KRW1,150 threshold, the options market has turned neutral on the won's short-term direction after shedding its bias for a won rise, and players are positioning for little movement either way in the spot market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The volatility implied by one-month at-the-money-forward dollar/won option prices has fallen to a year low of 6.70%/7.60% Monday from as high as 8.0%/9.0% in April, when the dollar was tumbling to KRW1,140.4 - within a whisker of its first break below KRW1,140 since November 2000. One-year implied volatility has edged down to 8.3%/9.6% from April's 9.5%/%10.5%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In terms of direction, one-month 25-delta risk reversals have been at 0% in recent weeks, signaling no bias in either direction. In April the one-month risk reversal moved as far as 1% in favor of dollar puts/won calls.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We saw similar spot prices back in April," when the dollar was testing KRW1,150, "but the sentiment behind the number then and now is very different," said K.H. Kang, chief options strategist at KorAm Bank. "People then all thought the won would eventually strengthen. These days, they aren't sure."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Econ Data Support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Stance \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the Korean authorities never tire of saying, the economic case for a strong won looks weak.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While exports have boomed, consumer spending has remained weak as households keep trying to recover from a credit-card shopping binge before the recession in the first half of 2003. A key barometer of consumer spending, sales in the retail and wholesale sectors, fell 2.2% on year in May, the biggest fall since January. And the Bank of Korea's consumer survey index, measuring consumers' economic outlook for the coming six months, marked its lowest reading in the second quarter since the fourth quarter of 2000.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The case for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  looks a lot stronger in the Korean market due to recent deterioration in sentiment," said Oh Suk-Tae, economist at Citibank. "This is in contrast to the rising optimism about the Japanese economy."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the dollar/won pair, finance ministry officials are keenly watching the won's movements against the yen, hoping to decouple the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the two Asian export powerhouses diverge economically. The yen has been stuck between KRW10.4 and KRW10.6 since late May, but many dealers predict the yen can revisit its April high of KRW11.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With domestic consumption "turning from bad to worse," said Yoon Yeo-Kwon, an official at the finance ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bureau, "it's undesirable for the won to track the yen when the economies of the two countries are going in different directions."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts argue the government can't defend the dollar as aggressively as before because rising oil prices are stoking inflationary pressures - which a weaker local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would only exacerbate. "We expect the finance ministry will recognize the risks of further inflation and allow the won to appreciate to choke off the cost-push inflation," said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Simon Flint at Bank of America.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the finance ministry's Choi insisted inflationary concerns won't affect the government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. "It's dangerous to think we can temper cost-push inflationary pressure through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government instead will use other tools to curb inflation, such as postponing a hike in public-service fees and seeking to cut mobile phone fees despite protests from wireless carriers, said Finance and Economy Minister Lee Hun-Jai.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If anyone's still wondering whether the Korean government will remain serious in its fight to tame the won, they need only consider this: the government wants to boost its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  limit next year by 49% from this year's planned total to KRW28.1 trillion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com \par    (Hye-Seung Seo has been covering the South Korean economy  with Dow Jones Newswires since 2000. Before Dow Jones, she covered the Korean economy and general news with domestic press Yonhap News Agency for eight years.) \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-07-04 0232GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1186 4252  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040705e0750001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
