\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Dollar holds firm grip on reserve  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  status.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2004\par 12:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 2 (Reuters) - Central banks often talk about diversifying their reserves away from dollars but there are few signs yet that the greenback is losing its status as the world's preferred stand-by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Figures from the Bank for International Settlements this week show the dollar accounted for 69 percent of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of last year, slightly below peaks seen in 2001.  \par \par \uc2 But factor in the dollar's 30 percent slide against the euro and 18 percent fall against the yen over that period and a different picture emerges. Not only are central banks amassing reserves in record amounts but they are bagging more dollars than before.  \par \par \uc2 "When you remove valuation effects, there has been a big increase in the dollar's proportion of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," said Marvin Barth, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 Barth notes there has been a positive correlation between the dollar's slide and the rise of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coffers.  \par \par \uc2 In 2000, when the dollar was pushing to 15-year highs against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , less than 27 percent of new reserve accumulation was in dollars. Last year, when the dollar was tumbling across the board, nearly 88 percent of new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings were in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 ASIAN ROAR  \par \par \uc2 A rush to buy dollars just as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tumbling may seem an odd investment decision. But central banks are not typical investors and making money plays second fiddle to broader economic goals.  \par \par \uc2 Central banks in export-reliant Asia have been particularly big buyers of dollars as authorities have sought to protect export competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen in 2003 to prevent appreciation against the dollar, while China has had to devour dollars to maintain its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.  \par \par \uc2 "The accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves reflects the sheer scale of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Asia, particularly \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 Official data show Asian central banks held $2.13 trillion of combined \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of March, up from $1.91 trillion at the end of 2003 and $1.44 trillion at the end of 2002.  \par \par \uc2 While more recent than those calculated by the BIS, these figures - released by the central banks - do not show a breakdown by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Apart from the BIS, the only other organisation to release such data is the IMF. Based on current rather than constant exchange rates, IMF data shows the dollar's share of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves slipped to 64.5 at the end of 2002 from 67.5 the previous year, while the euro's share edged up to 18.7 percent from 16.4.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF has yet to publish data for 2003.  \par \par \uc2 MAKING WAVES  \par \par \uc2 With more than $3 trillion in reserves, central banks are big hitters on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and their investment decisions can send shockwaves through the markets.  \par \par \uc2 While the BIS figures show little evidence of this happening, Asia central banks have repeatedly talked up plans to raise the euro component of their reserves.  \par \par \uc2 China - owner of the world's second largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves after Japan - has said it plans to include more European and Asian bonds in its reserves, while a Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters at the weekend he saw scope for further increases in the proportion of euro holdings. Russia said earlier this year it planned to move from a dollar-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  target to a basket comprising both dollars and euros, a move analysts say could have far-reaching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  implications.  \par \par \uc2 Russia has total reserves of around $88 billion, only a quarter of which are in euros, so a projected move to a 50/50 weighting could trigger additional euro buying to the tune of $22 billion, notes Michael Metcalfe, senior strategist at State Street.  \par \par \uc2 What would interest the market more, reckons Metcalfe, would be if Russia were to increase these reserves further by charging for its oil in euros.  \par \par \uc2 Europe is Russia's largest export market for oil and gas and such a move could set a precedent for other oil exporters, many of whom have already mooted the idea of pricing oil in euros rather than dollars.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040702e072000vo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
