\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Japan vows fiscal discipline as bond yields rise.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mari Terawaki and Chisa Fujioka  \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2004\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 2 (Reuters) - Japan needs strict fiscal discipline and credibility in its financial management to avoid unwarranted jumps in bond yields that could worsen the country's huge debt burden, new Vice Finance Minister Koichi Hosokawa said.  \par \par \uc2 "We need to be fully aware of the effect that (yields) have on debt costs and it's important that we maintain fiscal discipline and secure credibility in government bonds," he told Reuters in an interview earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 "We still have (a fiscal position) among the worst in the world and at crisis levels in historical terms," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Hosokawa, 56, took up the ministry's top post for bureaucrats on Friday, at a time when worries mount over the effect of rising government bond yields on Japan's ability to finance its record public debt.  \par \par \uc2 Signs of a firming economic recovery sent the 10-year Japanese government bond (JGB) yield to 1.920 percent earlier this month, the highest since September 2000. It was 1.795 percent on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Rising yields increase the cost of servicing Japan's some 703 trillion yen ($6.5 trillion) in outstanding debt as of March 31, about 140 percent of the value of the country's total economic output and the highest among industrialised countries.  \par \par \uc2 Higher servicing costs in turn cut the cash available to cover the state's other expenses at a time when its income from taxes has yet to pick up. Unless it cuts spending, it has to issue yet more bonds to make up the shortfall.  \par \par \uc2 To avoid excessive swings in yields, Hosokawa said the ministry would continue to do its part to make sure its massive debt issuance was absorbed by the market in a smooth manner.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry would continue to carry out plans set last December to strengthen debt management, he said.  \par \par \uc2 YIELDS A CONCERN  \par \par \uc2 Those plans included the introduction of a primary dealer system for the JGB market, measures to beef up market trend and risk analysis and increasing bond issues aimed at small investors.  \par \par \uc2 Hosokawa, formerly head of the ministry's budget bureau who lists reading and watching sumo wrestling among his hobbies, also voiced concern over the effect of rising yields on the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "An excessive rise (in yields) before a recovery in the real economy threatens to have a negative effect on economic growth," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's economic growth rose at the fastest pace in over a decade in the first three months of this year, but the government has stopped short of declaring a full recovery as some areas, such as small businesses, continue to struggle.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists have said rising yields could hurt business investment and the balance sheets of banks, who are big holders of JGBs, causing the recovery to slow.  \par \par \uc2 Hosokawa said the ministry would continue to work with the Bank of Japan (BOJ) to reverse a five-year decline in consumer prices, seen as an obstacle in sustaining economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 But he brushed off speculation that the ministry was pushing the BOJ to do more to boost the economy, saying only that its policies should be effective but what measures it took were ultimately for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself to decide.  \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said Japan stood by the statement the Group of Seven industrialised nations issued after a meeting in Boca Raton, Florida in February that excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates was undesirable, while also calling for exchange rate flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold 20 trillion yen last year and another 14 trillion in January-March to tame an export-threatening rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising the hackles of some of its trading partners, but has stayed away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no change to that view and we will continue to monitor the market and deal with matters accordingly," he said, declining to comment if that meant \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resume the controversial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy if the need arose. ($1=108.44 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040702e072000pp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
