\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:Wild Dlr Spot Rate Hurts Japan's Voice Brokers  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2004\par 07:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar's recent volatile swings have taken their toll on Japan's already embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  voice brokers, who are struggling to maintain a foothold in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has picked up after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended in March its long-running heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen from rising. In more choppy trading since then, market participants have tended to use electronic broking services.  \par \par \uc2 That is bad new for the voice brokers, who saw their share of dollar-yen spot trades in Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market slide to just 9.53% in May, their worst performance since early 2003, according to Hiroshi Takao, general manager of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar-yen's daily movement has recently been very choppy," Takao told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview. "That's not where voice brokering can be used."  \par \par \uc2 In a choppy market, "bank dealers have to use electronic brokering platforms to close positions quickly when their positions turn against them," Takao said.  \par \par \uc2 Last month's poor performance suggests that a decade-long decline in market share isn't over for the voice brokers in Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where dollar-yen trades total between $150 billion and $240 billion each month.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Voice Brokers Losing Share To Electronic Brokers \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since electronic brokering systems took off in the early 1990s, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  voice brokers have continued to lose ground in Japan's spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  players have become increasingly attracted to electronic brokers like Electronic Brokering System (EBS), which process trades more quickly and charge only one-third the transaction fees required by voice brokers, Takao said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Between 1995 and 2002, voice brokers' share of Tokyo's dollar-yen spot market fell to 9.2% from 71%, according to Japan's industry group Money Brokers Association.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since 1993, the number of Japanese companies offering spot-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  voice brokering services has shrunk to three from eight, partly through mergers and acquisitions to survive. Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  & Ueda Harlow is one of the three.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the number of broking firms has shrunk, the number of actual brokers in the spot market has shriveled to just 40 from around 300 in 1992, Takao estimates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In May, the dollar fluctuated by an average 96 sen in each Tokyo session, up from an average daily volatility of 50 sen between June 2003 and March 2004, according to Takao.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During that 10-month period, Tokyo voice brokers had a market share of 14% of spot deals, according to the Brokers Association.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When average daily volatility shrank to 38 sen in December, due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's hefty dollar-dollar buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , voice brokers' market share hit a four-year high of 19.3%, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over a 15-month period to March this year, Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold a total of Y35 trillion mostly for dollars although the dollar still fell to a low of Y103.40 by the end of March from Y118.63 at the start of 2003 before rebounding.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even though the dollar fell sharply during that time, its fall was "generally smooth", helping voice brokers compete against their electronic counterparts, Takao said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's bias clearly down then, they managed to quote dollar/yen prices for dealers as quickly as - or sometimes faster than - computers, Takao said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Voice Brokers Offer Market Insight \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Voice brokers have been able to cling on to their remaining market share for two main reasons.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants can learn about the condition of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market from voice brokers, according to Takao.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders at Japanese banks have continued to use voice brokers to "find out where the market is likely to head or how heated it is," he said. "They can feel it from the tone of a broker's voice."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These brokers also still dominate trading of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  derivatives, including forward contracts and options, which are too complex for computers to adequately process.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A dealer at a Japanese trust bank says his bank uses voice brokers because they sometimes offer a tighter bid-offer spread on dollar-yen trades than are available on electronic brokers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others say that while they predominantly use electronic brokers, voice brokers are still needed in the unlikely event that electronic brokering systems crash.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The brokers themselves are hoping that a clear direction for the dollar-yen exchange rate will soon emerge. The best scenario would be for the dollar to resume its descent and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to restart \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As long as the market remains confident that (the dollar) will artificially be supported at a certain level ahead, there tend to be fewer sharp rebounds," Takao said, making the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market smoother and a better environment for voice brokers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Shawn Schroter [ 02-07-04 0536GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040702e0720004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
