\par\par \b \uc2 AWSJ(7/2) Asian Mkts Show Little Reaction To US Rate Hike   \b0\par\par\uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2004\par 04:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 AWSJ(7/2) Asian Mkts Show Little Reaction To US Rate Hike   \par \par \uc2 (From THE ASIAN WALL STREET JOURNAL)   \par \par \uc2 By Phillip Day and Anusha Attygalle   \par \par \uc2 Singapore -- MARKETS around Asia shrugged off the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to raise interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point Wednesday, but not because the move was irrelevant to Asia.   \par \par \uc2 In fact, Asian markets have been trading for months in anticipation of the Fed action, demonstrating just how important U.S. interest-rate policy has become around the world.   \par \par \uc2 Fears of a U.S. rate increase started percolating through Asian financial markets toward the end of March, resulting in the first tentative sales of Asian stocks and bonds after a rally that had lasted about 18 months. Other factors -- fears of a too-sharp slowing of China's economic growth and high oil prices -- also contributed to the sales. But the changing interest-rate outlook was the main trigger for foreign investors, key players in Asian stock and bond markets.   \par \par \uc2 Higher U.S. rates may not have an obvious immediate effect on the profitability of a Thai seafood producer or a Korean supermarket chain, but they do have a dramatic effect on the buying habits of international investors. Three months ago, with money about to get more expensive, foreigners started selling Asian holdings. This 'liquidity-driven' buying and selling is 'a pretty fickle thing,' notes Mark Matthews, head of equity sales at CLSA Securities (Thailand) Ltd.   \par \par \uc2 When interest rates are low and money is relatively cheap, investors take more risks with it. Big investors such as hedge funds are more willing to use borrowed money to invest in relatively risky markets such as India and Thailand. That is one of the reasons stock market prices in local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms more than doubled last year in Bangkok and were up about 70% in Bombay, two of the world's top performing markets. Markets across Asia have benefited from low rates as foreign investors piled in.   \par \par \uc2 As talk of higher U.S. rates gathered steam, and as rates rose in Britain and Australia, sales by global investors picked up pace in Asian markets, despite growing economies and rising profits at Asian companies. Last year's leading indexes, Thailand's SET and Bombay's Sensex, each have now fallen by about 16% since the start of the year, making them two of the world's worst performers.   \par \par \uc2 Even Japan, Asia's best-performing market this year, has seen spotty foreign interest since March.   \par \par \uc2 That isn't to say a Fed rate rise is all bad news for Asia. Last year, foreign buying of Asian stocks added to pressure on Asian central banks trying to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too much against the dollar, and hurting trade prospects in the process. This year, as investor flows have reversed, Asian central banks have been able to step back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in three months, since the deluge of foreign cash into its markets started to slow.   \par \par \uc2 UBS economists estimate Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves (including Japan) grew by $17 billion in May this year, after growing an average of $80 billion every month last year.   \par \par \uc2 Few Asian central banks are expected to follow the Fed's lead immediately, as they look closer to home instead for cues on the strength of their own economies.   \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks in the past have tended to follow the Fed's lead on rates to keep their monetary policy in line with global trends. But given additional concerns about the region's economic growth from the possibility of a so-called hard landing in China and higher global oil prices, Asian central banks will be particularly cautious about tightening monetary policy this time.   \par \par \uc2 Many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the yen, are expected to continue gaining against the dollar, despite the higher yield now attached to U.S. assets after Wednesday's rate increase. Higher regional interest rates would make Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to traders, making the fight to keep the dollar from weakening too far even harder for the region's central banks to control.   \par \par \uc2 China may prove the exception, as expectations grow that it will raise lending rates for the first time in nine years. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still expected to keep short-term rates virtually at zero until next year despite the most bullish business sentiment in 13 years.   \par \par \uc2 Beijing has been under pressure to raise rates to cool an economy that grew 9.8% in the first quarter from a year earlier, and where deflation has turned into worrisome price rises in the past year.   \par \par \uc2 But some people downplay that scenario. 'I think China's interest rates should be kept stable as it involves the issue of exchange rates and economic development,' said Zhu Xinqiang, executive assistant president at Bank of China, one of the nation's big four commercial banks.   \par \par \uc2 -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020040702e0720025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
