\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Stocks End Mixed; Shrug Off U.S. Interest Rate Hike  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2004\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets around Asia ended mixed Thursday, shrugging off the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to raise interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point Wednesday, but not because the move was irrelevant to Asia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 In fact, Asian markets have been trading for months in anticipation of the Fed move, demonstrating just how important U.S. interest-rate policy has become around the world.  \par \par \uc2 Fears of a U.S. rate increase started percolating through Asian financial markets toward the end of March, resulting in the first tentative sales of Asian stocks and bonds after a rally that had lasted about 18 months. Other factors -- fears of a too-sharp slowing of Chinese growth and high oil prices -- also contributed to the sales. But the changing interest rate outlook was the main trigger for foreign investors, key players in Asian stock and bond markets.  \par \par \uc2 Higher U.S. rates may not have an obvious immediate effect on the profitability of a Thai seafood producer or a Korean supermarket chain, but they do have a dramatic effect on the buying habits of international investors. Three months ago, with money about to get more expensive, foreigners started selling Asian holdings. This "liquidity-driven" buying and selling is "a pretty fickle thing," notes Mark Matthews, head of equity sales at CLSA Securities (Thailand) Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 When interest rates are low and money is relatively cheap, investors take more risks with it. Big investors such as hedge funds are more willing to use borrowed money to invest in relatively risky markets like India and Thailand. That's one of the reasons stock market prices more than doubled last year in Bombay and Bangkok, two of the world's top performing markets. Markets across Asia have benefited from low rates as foreign investors piled in.  \par \par \uc2 As talk of higher U.S. rates gathered steam, and as rates rose in the U.K. and Australia, sales by global investors picked up pace in Asian markets, despite growing economies and rising profits at Asian companies. Last year's leaders, Thailand's SET and Bombay's Sensex, have now fallen by more than 15% and 17% respectively, making them two of the world's worst performers this year.  \par \par \uc2 Even Japan, Asia's best-performing market this year, has seen spotty foreign interest since March.  \par \par \uc2 That isn't to say a Fed hike is all bad news for Asia. Last year, foreign buying of Asian stocks added to pressure on Asian central banks trying to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too much against the dollar, and hurting trade prospects in the process. This year, as investor flows have reversed, Asian central banks have been able to step back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in three months, since the deluge of foreign cash into its markets started to slow.  \par \par \uc2 UBS economists estimate Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves (including Japan) grew by $17 billion in May this year, after growing an average of $80 billion every month last year.  \par \par \uc2 Few Asian central banks are expected to follow the Fed's lead immediately, as they look closer to home instead for clues on the strength of their own economies.  \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks in the past have tended to follow the Fed's lead on rates to keep their monetary policy in line with global trends. But given additional concerns about the region's economic growth from the possibility of a hard landing in China and higher global oil prices, Asian central banks will be particularly cautious about tightening monetary policy this time.  \par \par \uc2 Many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the yen, are expected to continue gaining against the dollar, despite the higher yield now attached to U.S. assets after Wednesday's Fed rate hike. Higher regional interest rates would make Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to traders, making the fight to keep the dollar from weakening too far even harder for the region's central banks to control.  \par \par \uc2 China may prove the exception, as expectations grow that it will raise lending rates for the first time in nine years. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still expected to keep short-term rates virtually at zero until next year despite the most bullish business sentiment in 13 years.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing has been under pressure to raise rates to cool an economy that grew 9.8% on year in the first quarter and where deflation has turned to worrying prices rises in the past year.  \par \par \uc2 But some are still downplaying the likelihood of a Chinese rate hike. "I think China's interest rates should be kept stable as it involves the issue of exchange rates and economic development," said Zhu Xinqiang, executive assistant president at Bank of China, one of the nation's big four commercial banks.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan on Thursday, after rising as high as 11988.12, the Nikkei 225 ended the day up 0.3%, or 37.14 points, at 11896.01. That was its highest close since April 28. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the dollar was at 108.11 yen late Thursday, slightly down from 108.83 late Wednesday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 In the U.S., the Dow Jones Industrial Average on Wednesday was up 22.05 to 10435.48.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's survey stole the spotlight from news of the U.S Federal Reserve's move to tighten monetary policy for the first time in four years. The BOJ news also led to buying of Toyota, Sony and other blue chips, which helped the Nikkei 225 briefly flirt with the psychologically key 12000 level.  \par \par \uc2 Toyota Motor set a new high for the year of 4,500 yen ($41.69), before pulling back to end up 1.6%. Sony rose 1.2% partly because the company said it will release a music player equipped with hard disk drives, which could rival Apple Computer's iPod, this month in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese companies have become the most upbeat about business conditions since the country's land-and-stock bubble burst more than a decade ago, the closely watched tankan survey found.  \par \par \uc2 In other news, Japanese car sales fell in June, declining from a year ago for the fifth straight month, the Japan Automobile Dealers Association said.  \par \par \uc2 In China, shares soared on a series of positive factors, including the Fed statement.  \par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index, which tracks A and B shares, surged 3% to end at 1441.07, its highest in a week and a half. The Shenzhen Composite Index soared 3.2% to close at 365.86.  \par \par \uc2 China Merchants Bank's A shares rose 2.2%, Shanghai Pudong Development Bank gained 2.5% and China Minsheng Banking Corp.'s A shares rallied 3.5%.  \par \par \uc2 In Taiwan, shares ended slightly lower after the Fed announcement.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan's largest flat-panel maker AU Optronics fell 1%, while smaller rival Chi Mei Optoelectronics dropped 1.9%.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean shares ended lower on profit-taking after the widely anticipated interest rate increase by the U.S. Federal Reserve, coupled with program selling, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong markets were closed for a bank holiday. [ 07-01-04 0800ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040701e0710000e\par }\page {\par\fs