\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan names Watanabe as  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  policy tsar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2004\par 10:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 25 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) appointed international policy veteran Hiroshi Watanabe as its new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy chief on Friday to succeed outgoing vice finance minister for international affairs Zembei Mizoguchi.  \par \par \uc2 Currently the head of the MOF's international bureau, Watanabe has been the righthand man for Mizoguchi, whose 1-= year stint saw a record 35 trillion yen ($327 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at preventing a higher yen from derailing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who turns 55 on Saturday, takes up the position at a time when speculation is mounting that the ministry may soon resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign after a three-month hiatus as the yen edges close to four-year highs against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Mizoguchi encountered a lot of difficult issues during his tenure. The issues won't just go away, and I expect him (Watanabe) to keep a lookout for a wide range of things as he carries out his tasks," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe's deputy, Kiyoto Ido, will replace him as the head of the international bureau. Koichi Hosokawa, currently the head of the budget bureau, will replace Masakazu Hayashi as vice finance minister, the top position for bureaucrats.  \par \par \uc2 The appointments take effect on July 2.  \par \par \uc2 Confident and soft-spoken, Watanabe is respected in the ministry and has been a vocal critic of what he calls excessive speculative trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Few expect him to pull any punches.  \par \par \uc2 "I am a nervous person and my heart is weak. Of course, how I look in other people's eyes is a different story," a chuckling Watanabe once said in a conversation with a Reuters reporter.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe has said on many occasions that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tool for authorities to smooth out abrupt movements in the markets. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can soften the waves washing onto the shoreline, although it may not change the current.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold some 15 trillion yen during market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first three months of the year, on top of a record 20 trillion yen last year.  \par \par \uc2 G7 BECKONS  \par \par \uc2 Such heavy market forays have drawn criticism from many of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade partners, but talk of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has grown recently as the yen scaled 10-week highs around 107 to the dollar helped by signs of solid economic growth in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 The yen is now within four percent of the four-year peaks around 103.40 set in April and up more than seven percent from a mid-May trough of around 115.  \par \par \uc2 A further rise could test Watanabe's mettle on the international stage ahead of a Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting in September.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe will be involved in the preparations for the G7 meeting, and the wording in G7 statements about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has always been contentious.  \par \par \uc2 "It goes without saying that we need him to collect a wide range of information from abroad. At the same time, we need to convey our thoughts to our overseas counterparts," Tanigaki said of Watanabe's mission.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe is no stranger to international negotiations.  \par \par \uc2 He served as an assistant to a former Finance Minister, Kiichi Miyazawa, helping in the $40 billion "Miyazawa Initiative" in 1998 to support troubled Asian countries after a region-wide economic crisis.  \par \par \uc2 At that time it was Watanabe's job to take phone calls for his boss from U.S. Treasury Lawrence Summers in the middle of the night in Tokyo so that the near-octogenarian Miyazawa need not be disturbed.  \par \par \uc2 Unlike his predecessors - Mizoguchi, Haruhiko Kuroda and Eisuke Sakakibara - Watanabe prefers to steer clear of news reporters huddled at the foyer of the ministry building every morning to ask policy makers about their view on exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, he has held informal forums with reporters, usually every week. Whether he will stick to that format remains to be seen as a rising yen may require more frequent communication with the market.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe is a big fan of mystery stories and has written a book using mysteries from around the world as a way to look into various cultures.  \par \par \uc2 In keeping with his fondness for mysteries, he is considered one of the architects of the MOF's so-called "stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", where it does not immediately confirm or deny its market action.  \par \par \uc2 Previously, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually disclosed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least when asked by the media. But the market was taken by surprise early last year to find out weeks later that the ministry had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  - quite heavily - in January.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040625e06p000sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
