\par\par \b \uc2 Japan names Watanabe as  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  policy tsar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2004\par 09:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 25 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) appointed international policy veteran Hiroshi Watanabe as its new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy chief on Friday to succeed outgoing vice finance minister for international affairs Zembei Mizoguchi.  \par \par \uc2 Currently the head of the MOF's international bureau, Watanabe has been the righthand man for Mizoguchi, whose 1-= year stint saw a record 35 trillion yen ($327 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at preventing a higher yen from derailing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe, who turns 55 on Saturday, takes up the position at a time when speculation is mounting that the ministry may soon resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign after a three-month hiatus as the yen edges close to four-year highs against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Confident and soft-spoken, he is respected in the ministry and has been a vocal critic of what he calls excessive speculative trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Few expect him to pull any punches.  \par \par \uc2 "I am a nervous person and my heart is weak. Of course, how I look in other people's eyes is a different story," a chuckling Watanabe once said in a conversation with a Reuters reporter.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe has said on many occasions that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tool for authorities to smooth out abrupt movements in the market. Likening it to tetrapods in a harbour, he says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can soften the waves washing onto the shoreline, although it may not change the current.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted 15 trillion yen worth of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first three months of the year, on top of a record 20 trillion yen last year.  \par \par \uc2 Such heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has drawn criticism from many of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade partners, but talk of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has grown as the yen scaled 10-week highs on Friday around 107 to the dollar , helped by signs of solid economic growth in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 The yen is now within four percent of the four-year peaks around 103.40 set in April and up more than seven percent from a mid-May trough of around 115.  \par \par \uc2 MIYAZAWA'S ASSISTANT  \par \par \uc2 Although a significant part of Watanabe's career in the ministry had been in the tax bureau, he served as an assistant to then Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, helping the minister in his $40 billion "Miyazawa Initiative" in 1998 to support troubled Asian countries after a region-wide economic crisis.  \par \par \uc2 At that time it was Watanabe's job to take phone calls for his boss from U.S. Treasury Lawrence Summers in the middle of the night in Tokyo so that the near-octogenarian Japanese minister need not be disturbed.  \par \par \uc2 In the mid-1970s, the MOF sent Watanabe to the United States to take a masters degree at Brown University.  \par \par \uc2 He is a big fan of mystery stories and has written a book using mysteries from around the world as a way to look into various cultures.  \par \par \uc2 In keeping with his fondness for mysteries, he is considered one of the architects of the MOF's so-called "stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", where it does not immediately confirm or deny its market action.  \par \par \uc2 Previously, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually disclosed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least when asked by the media. But the market was taken by surprise early last year to find out weeks later that the ministry had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  - quite heavily - in January.  \par \par \uc2 "We need to to have credibility in the market. But the market also needs a little surprise," he said in a recent informal chat with reporters that he holds almost every week.  \par \par \uc2 The surprise element of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long since gone, but the market will be on its toes for any new tricks that Watanabe might have up his sleeve.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040625e06p000n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
